#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""scripts/cdo-speed: the CDO speed and memory gate (docs/engine-limits.md L14).

Usage:
  scripts/cdo-speed <cdo-workspace> [--base REV] [--head REV] [--rounds N]
                    [--build-only] [--no-build] [--out DIR]
  scripts/cdo-speed --warm REV    (only build REV's binary into the cache; TLS-18:
                                   `scripts/merge-and-push` runs it after a landing, so
                                   the next gate finds its base cached)

It compares two `alsem` binaries, both built with the `release-fast` profile, on
a fixed set of CDO calls:
  head: the working tree (built in place, like every other gate), or `--head REV`;
  base: `--base REV`, by default the merge base of HEAD and `master`. When that
    is HEAD itself and the tree has no edits (a run on master), the default is
    HEAD's first parent instead, and the log says so: timing a tree against
    itself checks nothing.
A base binary is built from `git archive REV` and cached by commit under
`${ALSEM_SPEED_CACHE:-~/.cache/al-sem/cdo-speed}/bin/<sha>/`, so each master
commit is built once per machine, whatever worktree asks for it.

What runs: one `alsem mcp` server per side (the resident path agents use), asked
the same calls in turn (a fresh pair per warm-up pass; the last pair also runs the
rounds):
  warm-up (round 0, two passes, each on a fresh server pair; TLS-65): the first ask
    builds the CDO model (`build`), timed for each side with the other side's
    server closed (TLS-79: the other side's resident model made the second build
    cost x1.06-x1.29), then the first side's server is started again untimed;
    then one call of each tool builds the per-revision artifacts and the test
    universe (`warm-tests`). The second pass
    flips every call's side order, so each side is timed once going second (the
    second of a pair ran slower: ENG-81, ENG-97). Calibrated (TLS-79, 8 passes): per-pass
    build ratios x0.862-x1.029, a two-pass reading about ±8 %. Before each pass the host's CPU
    pressure (`some avg60`) must fall to CALM_PRESSURE; after CALM_WAIT_S (600 s) the
    pass runs anyway. Each timed block's CPU stall share (this container's `some total`
    over the block) is measured, and a block above CALM_PRESSURE (or a pass admitted by
    the timeout) labels the verdict "UNDER LOAD" with the shares: the same limits judge
    it, nothing is skipped (TLS-76). A host suspend before or during a timed call (the
    host's suspended seconds, `scripts/host-suspended`, rose) labels it "SUSPENDED" the
    same way (TLS-104: server1 is a laptop that sleeps when moved);
  rounds 1..N (default 3, at most 5, as many as ROUND_ROUTINES has; a larger --rounds is
    refused before anything starts): one CDO routine per round, a different one each round
    (a resident answer is kept, so asking the same routine again measures
    nothing), asked prove may-commit, explain, digest, query effects, tests and
    impact. Round 1 is RunReportID, the routine of the round-4 slowdown.
The side order flips every round, and in the warm-up every call, so neither side
always goes first.

What is judged, per call, head over base:
  rounds: CPU time (user + system of the server and its engine children, from
    /proc) and wall time; the median of the per-round ratios must be at most
    CPU_LIMIT and WALL_LIMIT. The round total (every call of a round summed) is
    judged the same way. These calls run on about one thread (CPU time is about
    their wall time), so their wall limit says little about lost parallelism;
  warm-up: one sample per pass, the ratio the geometric mean of the
    passes, only the calls of at least WARM_MIN_SECONDS (the model build and the
    test universe); the verdict line shows each pass's ratio and the load range.
    CPU time at most WARM_CPU_LIMIT, and
    wall time at most WARM_WALL_LIMIT: a wider limit, since a single sample's
    wall time swings on a loaded machine (up to x1.32 measured). These are the
    parallel parts (~160 s of CPU in ~36 s of wall for the model build, ~135 s
    in ~18 s for the test universe), so their wall time is where a change that
    loses parallelism (same CPU, more wall) shows;
  peak memory: the sum of the peak RSS (VmHWM) of every server process, each
    read after every call (an engine child that exits mid-run still counts if
    it was alive at any read; one that lives and dies inside a single call does
    not), at most MEM_LIMIT.
A call whose base CPU time is under MIN_SECONDS is judged in the round total only
(a ratio of two tiny times is mostly noise). The CHANGELOG's engine-limits wave 1,
L14 entry gives the measured noise behind each limit. When the rounds disagree
about a call (some ratios over its limit, some not), more rounds run, up to
--max-rounds; then the median decides.

Instructions (TLS-83). Each timed call also counts the user-mode instructions of
the server and its engine children (`perf stat` attached for the call; both core
types on a hybrid CPU, unscaled). They resolve far better than CPU seconds (the
model build: typically ±0.1 %, one 0.8 % jump in 12 builds, against ±8 % for a
two-pass CPU reading; measured n = 6), but they count work, not time: they do not see memory
stalls, cache misses or lost parallelism, so CPU and wall keep judging. Each
verdict line shows the call's instruction ratio, and the PASS/FAIL line the model
build's and the round total's. A ratio over INSTR_LIMIT (x1.02) is reported as an
ADVISORY and never fails the run: it becomes a failing limit only by an owner's
decision on data from real landings. Without perf (or CDO_SPEED_INSTR=0) the run
says "instructions: NOT counted" and judges CPU and wall alone.

Clock and cycles per instruction (TLS-111, docs/superpowers/notes/2026-11-21-tls111-
cpu-seconds-on-server1.md). On server1 CPU seconds measure the work times the clock,
core sharing and core kind, each able to move x2 between the sides of a pass. So
each call also counts user-mode cycles beside the instructions (per PMU; both fixed
counters, nothing else is allowed on a PMU; a PMU's two are one pinned perf group, so
they run exactly as long and are never time-sliced, and a PMU whose events did not, a
pinned group that got no counters, or a count whose running time does not cover the
call's CPU seconds, gives no count: see `perf_event_args`, `perf_parse`, `covers`) and
records user and system seconds apart. Per
side: the clock (user cycles / user seconds, GHz) and cycles per instruction. A judged
row whose sides' clocks differ beyond CLOCK_BAND is labelled CLOCK, one whose cycles
per instruction do beyond CPI_BAND, CPI (core sharing, core kind, or a real cache or
memory change: the label never says which). CPI never waives anything: more cycles
per instruction on equal instructions is what a cache or memory-stall regression looks
like. The CLOCK label changes one thing (owner decision q-20261121T025802-abcd2da8, the
owner may overrule; narrowed in TLS-111 review r3): a CPU-ONLY failure (the row's wall
passes) with equal instructions (within INSTR_LIMIT either way) is ADVISORY when head's
LOWER clock explains it: the CPU ratio at equal clocks, (head user x clock ratio + head
sys) / base CPU with clock ratio head/base, within the CPU limit, and in
a rounds row for every round over the limit by itself. It is printed with its CPU ratio,
the clock-corrected ratio and the instruction ratio. A clock in head's favour never
waives; wall and memory failures always fail. A count whose counters did not run for
the call's CPU seconds (`covers`) is recorded (`covered: false`, its counts kept) but
gives no instruction count (TLS-130), no clock, no label and so no advisory. A row
with any paired count that may be partial (a PMU `<not counted>`, or a kind's running
% under 100 summed over the PMUs: `partial_count`) gets no advisory either: its CPU
failure stays a failure, which says why (TLS-130 review r2). The advisory applies only
in the final judgment, after any extra pair.
A judged warm-up row that FAILS (advisory or not) while its two passes' ratios differ
beyond CONTRADICTED_SPREAD gets ONE more flipped pair (passes 3 and 4 on fresh servers,
each side order once more; each waits for a calm host as passes 1 and 2 do). Then every
(row, measure) that failed after pass 2 must agree within each side order (passes 1
and 3, 2 and 4, within the same band), and the geometric mean of all four passes
judges; a row judged after pass 2 stays judged. If any of them disagrees, the verdict
is HOST UNSTABLE: the run exits 2 (cannot run), never a PASS. The verdict says when a
pair was added. speed.json: `verdict` (PASS, FAIL or HOST UNSTABLE, which is in `failed`
too), `warm_samples` the passes run and `warm_samples_asked` the passes asked.

Output equality (the answer's hash) is reported (a branch may change answers on
purpose), never judged here: the CDO goldens do that. A call that exits non-zero
or fails in the engine, on either side, fails the run at once, naming the call,
its exit code and the first lines of its stderr: every call exits 0 on CDO, and
a ratio of failed calls (a wrong workspace answers in 0.01 s) measures nothing
(TLS-10: a run on a wrong workspace once printed PASS).

Exit: 0 pass, 1 fail (slower or larger, or a call failed), 2 cannot run (usage,
build, an MCP request that failed or timed out, HOST UNSTABLE). A run under load exits as any other,
labelled. Linux only (/proc).
"""
import argparse
import fcntl
import hashlib
import json
import os
import select
import shutil
import statistics
import subprocess
import sys
import signal
import tempfile
import time

# The limits, head over base. See the module doc.
CPU_LIMIT = 1.20
WALL_LIMIT = 1.30
# The warm-up's calls have one sample per pass: wider limits. Its wall limit is
# above the worst single sample against the same binary (x1.32: the model build
# beside a 22-core build; x1.31 beside 22 busy loops) and far below what losing
# their parallelism costs (on one core, from their CPU over wall: the model
# build about x4.4, the test universe about x7.5).
WARM_CPU_LIMIT = 1.30
WARM_WALL_LIMIT = 1.60
MEM_LIMIT = 1.10
MIN_SECONDS = 2.0
# A warm-up call is judged when its base CPU time is at least this: the model
# build (~160 s CPU) and the test universe (~135 s), not the 1-5 s calls whose
# single sample swung up to x1.19 against the same binary under load.
# (CDO_SPEED_WARM_MIN_SECONDS: a test hook, so a stub's short calls are judged.)
WARM_MIN_SECONDS = float(os.environ.get("CDO_SPEED_WARM_MIN_SECONDS", "20.0"))
# ⟨TLS-65⟩ The warm-up is timed twice, on two fresh server pairs, the second
# with every call's side order flipped: a call's ratio is the geometric mean of
# the two (it cancels a factor k on one pass and 1/k on the other exactly).
# One sample in a fixed order always timed head's model build second; the side
# built second was measured once at x1.167 for a binary against itself at load
# ~9 (ENG-81; not reproduced in TLS-65's own loaded run, x1.02), and one quiet
# TLS-65 run read x1.096 with head second against x1.004 flipped.
WARM_SAMPLES = 2
# ⟨TLS-111⟩ A judged row whose sides' clocks (user cycles / user seconds) or
# cycles per instruction differ beyond these bands (either way) is labelled CLOCK
# or CPI; the verdict does not change. Provisional: set after ~20 landings
# (docs/superpowers/notes/2026-11-21-tls111-cpu-seconds-on-server1.md §5.1).
CLOCK_BAND = 1.15
CPI_BAND = 1.15
# ⟨TLS-111⟩ A judged warm-up row that FAILS with its two passes disagreeing
# beyond this (max/min of the ratios) gets one more flipped pair (note §5.2).
# History (150 speed.json files, note §4): pass-to-pass disagreement median
# x1.06, p90 x1.23, p95 x1.80, max x2.67; an unchanged tree cannot fail the
# x1.30 mean below x1.69. The same band decides whether the extra pair's
# same-order passes agree.
CONTRADICTED_SPREAD = 1.25
# ⟨TLS-76⟩ CPU pressure, not the 1-min load average (TLS-65's signal), which
# counts other projects' runnable work on this shared host: it read 50-230 while
# the CPU was ~78 % idle and stopped every gate (owner q-20261115T181612-b2891501).
# Two uses (reviews r1, Fable and sol: a cgroup's pressure counts only its own
# tasks' stalls, so an idle container reads ~0 whatever the host does):
# - Admission: before each warm-up pass, the HOST's `some avg60`
#   (`/proc/pressure/cpu`; a minute, so one dip in bursty load does not pass) must
#   be at most CALM_PRESSURE; after CALM_WAIT_S the pass runs anyway. This only
#   lowers the odds of a loaded run.
# - The label: the share of each timed block (each warm-up pass, the rounds) in
#   which some task of THIS container waited for a CPU, from the cgroup's
#   `some total=` stall microseconds at its start and end (exact over the block,
#   no averaging window). A block above CALM_PRESSURE, or a pass admitted only by
#   the timeout, labels the verdict "UNDER LOAD" with the shares; the same limits
#   judge it (principle 6: label, never hide; 9: never weaken a gate).
# Calibration: see the CHANGELOG's TLS-76 entry.
CALM_PRESSURE = float(os.environ.get("CDO_SPEED_CALM_PRESSURE", "15"))
CALM_WAIT_S = float(os.environ.get("CDO_SPEED_CALM_WAIT_S", "600"))
CALM_POLL_S = float(os.environ.get("CDO_SPEED_CALM_POLL_S", "10"))
HOST_PRESSURE = "/proc/pressure/cpu"
OWN_PRESSURE = ("/sys/fs/cgroup/cpu.pressure", "/proc/pressure/cpu")


TEST_HOOKS = ("CDO_SPEED_WARM_MIN_SECONDS", "CDO_SPEED_LOADAVG_FILE", "CDO_SPEED_PRESSURE_FILE",
              "CDO_SPEED_HOST_PRESSURE_FILE", "CDO_SPEED_CALM_PRESSURE", "CDO_SPEED_CALM_WAIT_S",
              "CDO_SPEED_CALM_POLL_S", "CDO_SPEED_PERF", "CDO_SPEED_TEST_WALL_IS_CPU",
              "CDO_HOST_SUSPENDED_FILE")
# CDO_SPEED_TEST_WALL_IS_CPU: a test hook, a stub clock: each call's wall time is
# taken to be its CPU time. A stub server burns exact CPU, but its wall time is
# the host's, so under load a stub run could fail its wall limit (x1.78 against
# 1.6, TLS-103) whatever the test meant to check. A real gate never sets it.
# A stub may add wall-only seconds it declares as `stubSleptS` in its answer
# (TLS-111 review r5: tests of wall-time rules failed when a peer's CDO gate
# stretched real stub sleeps by seconds); `alsem` never sends that field.
TEST_WALL_IS_CPU = os.environ.get("CDO_SPEED_TEST_WALL_IS_CPU") == "1"


def load_1min():
    """The 1-min load average, recorded only (CDO_SPEED_LOADAVG_FILE: a test hook read instead)."""
    path = os.environ.get("CDO_SPEED_LOADAVG_FILE")
    if path:
        with open(path) as f:
            return float(f.read().split()[0])
    return os.getloadavg()[0]


def psi_some(paths, key):
    """The `some` line's `key` (`avg10`, `avg60`, `total`) from the first readable
    PSI file of `paths`, as (value, path), or (None, None)."""
    for path in paths:
        try:
            with open(path) as f:
                for line in f:
                    fields = line.split()
                    if fields and fields[0] == "some":
                        for kv in fields[1:]:
                            if kv.startswith(key + "="):
                                return float(kv[len(key) + 1:]), path
        except (OSError, ValueError):
            continue
    return None, None


def host_files():
    hook = os.environ.get("CDO_SPEED_HOST_PRESSURE_FILE")
    return [hook] if hook else [HOST_PRESSURE]


def own_files():
    hook = os.environ.get("CDO_SPEED_PRESSURE_FILE")
    return [hook] if hook else list(OWN_PRESSURE)


def cpu_pressure():
    """This container's `some avg10` (recorded per call), as (value, path)."""
    return psi_some(own_files(), "avg10")


class Stalls:
    """This container's CPU stall share over one timed block: the `some total=`
    microseconds at its start and end, over its wall time."""

    def __init__(self):
        self.total0, _ = psi_some(own_files(), "total")
        self.t0 = time.monotonic()

    def share(self):
        """The share in percent, or None when no total can be read."""
        total, _ = psi_some(own_files(), "total")
        wall = time.monotonic() - self.t0
        if total is None or self.total0 is None or wall <= 0:
            return None
        return 100.0 * (total - self.total0) / 1e6 / wall


# ⟨TLS-104⟩ Host suspends. server1 is a laptop that suspends when it is moved
# (owner, 2026-11-20): during ENG-145's landing it slept 1,965 s between two
# calls. A call's wall time is CLOCK_MONOTONIC, which stops during a suspend, so
# the call itself read right (8.2 s) while the gate's `$SECONDS` jumped 33 min;
# but the call after a resume runs with cold caches and clocks (that one took
# x2.0 its pair). So each timed call reads the host's suspended seconds
# (`host_suspended`) before and after it: a rise since the previous reading
# (before the call) or over the call is logged with its size and labels the
# verdict SUSPENDED. The same limits judge it (label, never hide); instruction
# counts are unaffected (a suspended task executes nothing).
SUSPEND_MIN_S = 1


def host_suspended():
    """The host's suspended seconds since boot (CLOCK_BOOTTIME minus
    CLOCK_MONOTONIC), or None when unreadable. CDO_HOST_SUSPENDED_FILE: a test
    hook, a file whose first number is read instead (as `scripts/host-suspended`)."""
    hook = os.environ.get("CDO_HOST_SUSPENDED_FILE")
    try:
        if hook:
            with open(hook) as f:
                return float(f.read().split()[0])
        # MONOTONIC first, never below 0 (as `host-suspended`; review r4, sol).
        m = time.clock_gettime(time.CLOCK_MONOTONIC)
        return max(0.0, time.clock_gettime(time.CLOCK_BOOTTIME) - m)
    except (OSError, ValueError, IndexError, AttributeError):
        return None


class Suspends:
    """The host suspends seen over the timing: (where, seconds) each."""

    def __init__(self):
        self.seen = []
        self.last = host_suspended()

    def note(self, where):
        """Read again; a rise of at least SUSPEND_MIN_S since the last reading is
        a suspend `where` (logged). Returns its seconds, or 0."""
        now = host_suspended()
        rise = 0
        if now is not None and self.last is not None and now - self.last >= SUSPEND_MIN_S:
            rise = now - self.last
            self.seen.append((where, rise))
            log(f"host suspended {rise:.0f} s {where}: its timing is not trusted "
                "(labelled SUSPENDED; instruction counts are unaffected)")
        if now is not None:
            self.last = now
        return rise

    def label(self):
        """` SUSPENDED: ...` when the host slept during the timing, else ``."""
        if not self.seen:
            return ""
        total = sum(s for _, s in self.seen)
        where = ", ".join(f"{s:.0f} s {w}" for w, s in self.seen)
        return (f" SUSPENDED: the host slept {total:.0f} s during the timing ({where}); "
                "the timings around it are not trusted, instruction counts are")


SUSPENDS = None


def shares_text(blocks):
    """`warm-up 1 3.2 %, ...` (or "not readable" for a block with no total)."""
    return ", ".join(f"{name} {'not readable' if s is None else f'{s:.1f} %'}"
                     for name, s in blocks) or "none timed"


def under_load_label(calm, blocks):
    """The verdict's label: " UNDER LOAD: ..." when a timed block's stall share
    passed CALM_PRESSURE or a pass was admitted only by the timeout; a note when
    the pressure could not be read; else ""."""
    high = [name for name, s in blocks if s is not None and s > CALM_PRESSURE]
    timed_out = [i + 1 for i, (_, loaded) in enumerate(calm) if loaded]
    if high or timed_out:
        why = []
        if high:
            why.append(f"stall share above {CALM_PRESSURE:.1f} % in {', '.join(high)}")
        if timed_out:
            why.append(f"host pressure above {CALM_PRESSURE:.1f} % for {CALM_WAIT_S:.0f} s "
                       f"before warm-up pass(es) {', '.join(map(str, timed_out))}")
        return f" UNDER LOAD: {'; '.join(why)} (shares: {shares_text(blocks)})"
    if any(loaded is None for _, loaded in calm) or any(s is None for _, s in blocks):
        return f" (CPU pressure not fully readable: shares {shares_text(blocks)})"
    return ""


def row_labels(row):
    """⟨TLS-111⟩ {"clock": ratio, "cpi": ratio} for the measures of `row` whose
    two sides differ beyond CLOCK_BAND or CPI_BAND, either way."""
    out = {}
    for m, band in (("clock", CLOCK_BAND), ("cpi", CPI_BAND)):
        r = row.get(m, {}).get("ratio")
        if r and max(r, 1 / r) > band:
            out[m] = r
    return out


def clock_cpi_flags(rows):
    """⟨TLS-111⟩ {"clock": [...], "cpi": [...]}: the judged rows (`call xR`) whose
    sides' clock or cycles per instruction differ beyond CLOCK_BAND or CPI_BAND."""
    flags = {"clock": [], "cpi": []}
    for row in rows:
        if row["judged"]:
            for m, r in row_labels(row).items():
                flags[m].append(f"{row['call']} x{r:.3f}")
    return flags


# ⟨TLS-111⟩ Owner decision (kraken, q-20261121T025802-abcd2da8; the owner may
# overrule), as narrowed in review r3 (Fable): a CPU-time failure is ADVISORY
# only when a LOWER CLOCK on head explains it. User CPU is about instructions x
# cycles per instruction / clock, so with equal instructions the excess comes
# from the clock or from more cycles per instruction; more cycles per
# instruction is exactly what a cache or memory-stall regression looks like, so
# a CPI label never waives anything. The conditions, all of them:
# - a CLOCK label on the row, from counts that cover the call (`covers`);
# - instructions equal (within INSTR_LIMIT either way);
# - the row's wall passes (a CPU-ONLY failure);
# - the clock explains the excess: the CPU ratio at equal clocks is within the
#   CPU limit. The clock is user cycles per USER second, so only head's user time
#   is scaled (review r4, sol): (head user x clock ratio + head sys) / (base user
#   + base sys), clock ratio = head/base GHz, below 1 when head ran slower. A
#   clock in head's favour (above 1) makes it larger, so it never waives. Per
#   pair (`judge`'s `corrected`): a warm-up row takes the geometric mean over its
#   passes, as its other ratios do;
# - head's clock is the slower one: the row's clock ratio below 1 (a warm-up
#   row's geometric mean, or each over-limit round's own). The corrected mean
#   alone is not enough (review r5, sol): passes at x0.60 (head 95 % user) and
#   x2.40 (head 10 % user) read an aggregate clock of x1.20 in head's favour
#   while the corrected CPU reads x1.18 and would waive;
# - for a rounds row, every round whose CPU ratio is over the limit meets the
#   instruction, wall and clock-corrected conditions by itself (medians taken
#   from different rounds may not combine; review r3, S2);
# - (TLS-130 reviews r1 and r2, sol and Fable) every paired count of the row,
#   on both sides, is whole and covers its call (`judge`'s `partial_rounds`:
#   `partial_count` finds no `<not counted>` PMU and each kind's running % summed
#   to at least 100; `covers`; a rounds row checks the rounds it judges). A count
#   that may be partial can move the instruction ratio, the clock and the CLOCK
#   label by an amount no rule here can bound (the lost time need not have run
#   at the call's average rate), so such a row keeps its CPU failure as
#   measured and says why (`row["partial"]`). Not seen (review r3, Fable): a
#   loss inside a counted row under the coverage band (the inherited-thread
#   shape, see `covers`); real multi-threaded calls sum their running % to
#   110-125, so the running-% rule binds only on a call on one core type.
# Wall and memory failures always fail. The advisory applies only in the final
# judgment: a contradicted failure gets the extra pair first (`warm_failures`
# ignores the advisory).
CPU_ADVISORY_QUESTION = "q-20261121T025802-abcd2da8"


def cpu_advisory(row):
    """The advisory text when `row`'s CPU-time failure is advisory, else None."""
    i, c, k = row.get("instr"), row.get("clock"), row.get("corrected")
    warm = row.get("warm", False)
    cpu_limit = WARM_CPU_LIMIT if warm else CPU_LIMIT
    wall_limit = WARM_WALL_LIMIT if warm else WALL_LIMIT
    if not (i and c and k and "clock" in row_labels(row)) or row["wall"]["ratio"] > wall_limit:
        return None
    # ⟨TLS-130 reviews r2 and r3⟩ A count `judge` takes as partial or not
    # covering (`partial_rounds`) gives no advisory, in every pass a warm-up
    # row's mean takes and in every round a rounds row judges (over the limit).
    partial = row.get("partial_rounds") or [row.get("partial")] * len(row["cpu"]["ratios"])
    # (CPU, instructions, CPU at equal clocks, wall, clock, partial) per sample:
    # the warm-up row's judged (geometric mean) ratios, or each over-limit round
    # of a rounds row.
    if warm:
        samples = [(row["cpu"]["ratio"], i["ratio"], k["ratio"], row["wall"]["ratio"],
                    c["ratio"], "; ".join(p for p in partial if p))]
    else:
        samples = [s for s in zip(row["cpu"]["ratios"], i["ratios"], k["ratios"],
                                  row["wall"]["ratios"], c["ratios"], partial)
                   if s[0] > cpu_limit]
    for cpu, ir, corrected, w, cr, part in samples:
        if part or not (1 / INSTR_LIMIT <= ir <= INSTR_LIMIT and w <= wall_limit
                        and corrected <= cpu_limit and cr < 1):
            return None
    corrected = k["ratio"]
    return (f"{row['call']}: CPU time x{row['cpu']['ratio']:.2f}, x{corrected:.2f} at equal "
            f"clocks (CLOCK x{c['ratio']:.3f}), with instructions x{i['ratio']:.4f} "
            f"(advisory, owner decision {CPU_ADVISORY_QUESTION})")


def warm_failures(row):
    """The limits a judged warm-up row fails: [(metric, ratios)]. A CPU failure
    that the final judgment may make advisory counts here too (review r2: a
    contradicted failure gets the extra pair before any advisory)."""
    out = []
    if not row["judged"]:
        return out
    for m, lim in (("cpu", WARM_CPU_LIMIT), ("wall", WARM_WALL_LIMIT)):
        if row[m]["ratio"] > lim:
            out.append((m, row[m]["ratios"]))
    return out


def clock_cpi_label(flags):
    """The verdict's ` CLOCK: ...` / ` CPI: ...` labels (TLS-111), else ``."""
    label = ""
    if flags["clock"]:
        label += (f" CLOCK: the sides' clocks (user cycles / user s) differ beyond "
                  f"x{CLOCK_BAND} on {', '.join(flags['clock'])}")
    if flags["cpi"]:
        label += (f" CPI: the sides' cycles per instruction differ beyond x{CPI_BAND} on "
                  f"{', '.join(flags['cpi'])} (core sharing, core kind, or a real cache or "
                  "memory change)")
    return label


def judge_warm(records, keep_judged=()):
    """The warm-up's rows: one sample per pass, paired by pass; each ratio the
    geometric mean over the passes (with the flipped ones, a factor k on one and
    1/k on the other cancels exactly; an arithmetic mean would lean against head).
    `keep_judged`: calls judged after pass 2 stay judged on the extra pair's
    samples, whatever their base CPU then (TLS-111 review r1)."""
    warm_records = [r for r in records if r["round"] == 0]
    warm_rows, _ = judge([dict(r, round=r["warm_pass"] + 1) for r in warm_records])
    warm_rows = [r for r in warm_rows if r["call"] != "round-total"]
    for row in warm_rows:
        for m in ("cpu", "wall", "instr", "clock", "cpi", "corrected"):
            if m not in row:
                continue
            ratios = row[m]["ratios"]
            if ratios and min(ratios) > 0:
                row[m]["ratio"] = statistics.geometric_mean(ratios)
        loads = [r["load"] for r in warm_records if r["call"] == row["call"]]
        row["load"] = dict(min=min(loads), max=max(loads))
        row["warm"] = True
        row["judged"] = (row["cpu"]["base"] >= WARM_MIN_SECONDS
                         or row["call"] in keep_judged)
    return warm_rows


def failing(warm_rows):
    """⟨TLS-111⟩ Every judged warm-up (call, metric) that fails, a CPU failure
    the final judgment may make advisory included: [(call, metric, ratios)]."""
    return [(row["call"], m, rs) for row in warm_rows for m, rs in warm_failures(row)]


def contradicted(fails):
    """⟨TLS-111⟩ The failures of `failing` whose passes disagree beyond
    CONTRADICTED_SPREAD (max/min of the ratios): they add the extra pair."""
    return [(c, m, rs) for c, m, rs in fails
            if min(rs) <= 0 or max(rs) / min(rs) > CONTRADICTED_SPREAD]


def same_order_disagree(warm_rows, fails):
    """⟨TLS-111⟩ After the extra pair: of EVERY (call, metric) that failed after
    pass 2 (not only those that added the pair: review r1, sol and Fable, the
    four-pass mean may clear a failure only where the passes agree), those whose
    two passes of one side order (passes 1 and 3, or 2 and 4) differ beyond
    CONTRADICTED_SPREAD, as text; empty when every one agrees."""
    out = []
    for call, m, _ in fails:
        rs = next(r for r in warm_rows if r["call"] == call)[m]["ratios"]
        for i in (0, 1):
            a, b = rs[i], rs[i + 2]
            if min(a, b) <= 0 or max(a, b) / min(a, b) > CONTRADICTED_SPREAD:
                out.append(f"{call} {m} passes {i + 1} and {i + 3} x{a:.3f}/x{b:.3f}")
    return out


def wait_calm(what):
    """Wait until the host's `some avg60` is at most CALM_PRESSURE. Returns (the
    pressure then, True when the pass was admitted only by the CALM_WAIT_S timeout,
    None when no host pressure can be read)."""
    start = time.monotonic()
    said = False
    while True:
        p, source = psi_some(host_files(), "avg60")
        if p is None:
            log(f"{what}: no host CPU pressure readable ({', '.join(host_files())}): not checked")
            return None, None
        if p <= CALM_PRESSURE:
            if said:
                log(f"{what}: host CPU pressure {p:.1f} % (avg60, {source}), calm enough after "
                    f"{time.monotonic() - start:.0f} s")
            return p, False
        waited = time.monotonic() - start
        if waited >= CALM_WAIT_S:
            log(f"{what}: host CPU pressure {p:.1f} % (avg60) stayed above {CALM_PRESSURE:.1f} % "
                f"for {waited:.0f} s: running anyway, UNDER LOAD")
            return p, True
        if not said:
            log(f"{what}: host CPU pressure {p:.1f} % (avg60, {source}) is above "
                f"{CALM_PRESSURE:.1f} %: waiting (up to {CALM_WAIT_S:.0f} s)")
            said = True
        time.sleep(CALM_POLL_S)

RRID = "CDO E-Mail Handler::RunReportID"
ISSUE = "CDO Issue Document::IssueReminder"
TELEMETRY = "CDO Telemetry::UpdateLastUpload"

# One routine per round (round 1 first); --max-rounds is at most this many.
# Each one's first prove costs 10-20 s on master (release-fast, 2026-11-09): a
# routine whose answer takes 1-2 s says little about a slowdown.
ROUND_ROUTINES = [
    RRID,
    ISSUE,
    "CDO E-Mail Handler::DocEMailSend",
    "CDO E-Mail Handler::DocSave",
    "CDO E-Mail Handler::RunReport",
]


def calls_for(routine):
    """(id, tool, arguments) for one routine. Prove first: explain reuses its search."""
    q = routine.replace("::", ".")
    return [
        ("prove", "alsem_prove",
         {"routine": routine, "question": "may-commit", "deterministic": True}),
        ("explain", "alsem_explain", {"routines": [routine]}),
        ("digest", "alsem_digest", {"routines": [routine], "deterministic": True}),
        ("query", "alsem_query_effects", {"routine": q, "deterministic": True}),
        ("tests", "alsem_tests", {"routines": [routine]}),
        ("impact", "alsem_impact", {"routines": [routine]}),
    ]


# Round 0: `build` is the first ask (the model); the rest build what each tool
# needs once per revision (the test universe for `tests`).
WARM_UP = [("build", "alsem_explain", {"routines": [TELEMETRY]})] + [
    (f"warm-{cid}", tool, args) for cid, tool, args in calls_for(TELEMETRY)]

CALL_TIMEOUT = 900
TICK = os.sysconf("SC_CLK_TCK") if hasattr(os, "sysconf") else 100


def log(msg):
    print(f"cdo-speed: {msg}", flush=True)


def git(repo, *args):
    return subprocess.run(["git", "-C", repo, *args], check=True, capture_output=True,
                          text=True).stdout.strip()


def timed_hold(repo):
    """⟨TLS-89⟩ Mark the CDO slot's hold timed for the rest of this run (after its
    builds, before the first timed call), so no build runs beside the timing: the
    guard refuses new ones, and cdo-slot waits for running ones and refuses (exit 6)
    when they do not end. Inside a hold (cdo-gate's or land's token, `cdo-slot run`,
    or this Claude session's hand hold) it shares it; outside any it takes the slot
    itself, waiting its turn (review r1, sol: a run with no token went unguarded).
    Bound to this process: the mark, and a hold it took, end with it."""
    rc = subprocess.run(["bash", os.path.join(repo, "scripts", "cdo-slot"), "acquire",
                         os.environ.get("CDO_SLOT_OWNER", "cdo-speed"), "cdo-speed timing",
                         "--wait", "--bind", str(os.getpid()), "--timed"]).returncode
    if rc == 6:
        log("builds still ran when the timing was to begin (scripts/cdo-slot --timed): "
            "timed nothing")
        sys.exit(2)
    if rc not in (0, 4):
        log(f"the timed CDO slot hold was not taken (cdo-slot exit {rc})")
        sys.exit(2)


# ⟨ENG-110⟩ The dependency IR cache's variables are kept on purpose (every other ALSEM_*
# is stripped); ALSEM_IR_CACHE_VERIFY is refused at the start (`main`).
IR_CACHE_VARS = ("ALSEM_IR_CACHE", "ALSEM_IR_CACHE_DIR", "ALSEM_IR_CACHE_REPORT")


def child_env(extra=None):
    """Nothing that changes the engine's behaviour or adds tracing cost. ⟨ENG-110⟩ The
    judged rows run with the IR cache off on both sides (`ALSEM_IR_CACHE=off`), so
    dependency parsing stays timed; `extra` turns it on for the reported IR rows."""
    env = {k: v for k, v in os.environ.items()
           if (not k.startswith("ALSEM_") or k in IR_CACHE_VARS) and k != "C1_CONE_CENSUS"}
    env["ALSEM_IR_CACHE"] = "off"
    env.update(extra or {})
    return env


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Binaries
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

def target_dir(repo):
    return os.environ.get("CARGO_TARGET_DIR") or os.path.join(repo, "target")


def gate_release_dir(repo):
    """The folder `scripts/ci-steps`' optimized build (`build` / `perf-bounds`) uses:
    `<target>/gate-release`, spelled as ci-steps spells it (TLS-09)."""
    return os.path.join(target_dir(repo), "gate-release")


def build_working_tree(repo, logf):
    """Build the working tree's alsem (release-fast) in ci-steps' optimized build folder;
    return its path. ⟨TLS-33⟩ A landing gate runs `ci-steps all` first, which builds the
    same profile and features there, so cargo finds every artifact fresh and builds nothing
    (in the main target it rebuilt al-sem: 255 s on cdo-gate's critical path). An edit
    since is rebuilt by cargo's own freshness check; with no ci-steps build first (a
    scheduled run) it builds as before, in that folder. ci-steps' build and this one never
    run at once (`ci-steps all` ends before `cdo-gate` starts), and cargo's build-folder
    lock would make the second wait if they did."""
    log("building the working tree (release-fast, alsem)")
    t0 = time.monotonic()
    env = dict(os.environ)
    env["CARGO_TARGET_DIR"] = gate_release_dir(repo)
    rc = subprocess.run(["cargo", "build", "--profile", "release-fast", "--bin", "alsem"],
                        cwd=repo, env=env, stdout=logf, stderr=subprocess.STDOUT).returncode
    if rc != 0:
        raise SystemExit(f"cdo-speed: the working tree's build failed (log: {logf.name})")
    log(f"  built in {time.monotonic() - t0:.0f} s")
    return os.path.join(gate_release_dir(repo), "release-fast", "alsem")


def default_base(repo, head_rev):
    """The merge base of HEAD and master; on master itself with no edits (a
    scheduled run, or the post-merge check at the merge commit) that is the tree
    being timed, so HEAD's first parent instead."""
    base = git(repo, "merge-base", "HEAD", "master")
    if head_rev is not None or base != git(repo, "rev-parse", "HEAD"):
        return base
    if git(repo, "status", "--porcelain", "--untracked-files=no"):
        log("the merge base is HEAD; timing the edits against HEAD")
        return base
    parent = git(repo, "rev-parse", "--verify", "HEAD^1")
    log("****************************************************************")
    log("**  HEAD is the merge base and the tree has no edits: timing    **")
    log(f"**  HEAD against its first parent {parent[:10]} instead.       **")
    log("****************************************************************")
    return parent


def cache_root():
    return os.environ.get("ALSEM_SPEED_CACHE") or os.path.join(
        os.path.expanduser("~"), ".cache", "al-sem", "cdo-speed")


TREE_ENTRY_DAYS = 7


def grammar_dir(repo, archive):
    """The grammar clone a build uses: `TREE_SITTER_AL_PATH` when set; else the
    working tree's own `tree-sitter-al` (al-syntax's build.rs fallback) or, for an
    archive build (`cached_binary`), the main checkout's."""
    if os.environ.get("TREE_SITTER_AL_PATH"):
        return os.environ["TREE_SITTER_AL_PATH"]
    if archive:
        return os.path.normpath(os.path.join(
            git(repo, "rev-parse", "--path-format=absolute", "--git-common-dir"),
            "..", "tree-sitter-al"))
    return os.path.join(repo, "tree-sitter-al")


# Environment that changes what a build compiles, beyond the sources and the
# grammar: any of these set means no key (review r1-r2, sol).
BUILD_ENV_EXACT = ("RUSTFLAGS", "CARGO_ENCODED_RUSTFLAGS", "RUSTC", "RUSTC_WRAPPER",
                   "RUSTC_WORKSPACE_WRAPPER", "CC", "CXX", "CFLAGS", "CXXFLAGS", "CPPFLAGS",
                   "LDFLAGS", "AR",
                   # cc-rs's host and target spellings (review r3, sol); by name, as a
                   # `HOST_` prefix would also catch `HOSTNAME`.
                   "HOST_CC", "HOST_CXX", "HOST_CFLAGS", "HOST_CXXFLAGS", "HOST_AR",
                   "TARGET_CC", "TARGET_CXX", "TARGET_CFLAGS", "TARGET_CXXFLAGS", "TARGET_AR",
                   "CRATE_CC_NO_DEFAULTS",
                   # Review r1, Opus: another compiler than the tree's pin, incremental
                   # code in release-fast, a value the root build.rs bakes into the binary.
                   "RUSTUP_TOOLCHAIN", "CARGO_INCREMENTAL", "AL_CH_TELEMETRY_CONNECTION_STRING")
BUILD_ENV_PREFIXES = ("CARGO_PROFILE_", "CARGO_BUILD_", "CARGO_TARGET_", "CFLAGS_",
                      "CXXFLAGS_", "CC_", "CXX_", "AR_", "CARGO_ENCODED_RUSTFLAGS")
# Where and how fast a build runs, not what it compiles.
BUILD_ENV_HARMLESS = ("CARGO_TARGET_DIR", "CARGO_BUILD_TARGET_DIR", "CARGO_BUILD_JOBS")


def build_overrides(repo):
    """What, besides the sources and the grammar, could change a build here: build
    environment, or a Cargo config file in a folder above the repo (the repo's own
    `.cargo` is in its tree; the user's `$CARGO_HOME` applies to every build alike).
    Empty when there is none."""
    found = [k for k in os.environ if os.environ[k] and k not in BUILD_ENV_HARMLESS and (
        k in BUILD_ENV_EXACT or k.startswith(BUILD_ENV_PREFIXES))]
    cargo_home = os.path.realpath(os.environ.get("CARGO_HOME")
                                  or os.path.join(os.path.expanduser("~"), ".cargo"))
    d = os.path.dirname(os.path.realpath(repo))
    while True:
        for name in ("config", "config.toml"):
            p = os.path.join(d, ".cargo", name)
            if os.path.exists(p) and os.path.realpath(os.path.dirname(p)) != cargo_home:
                found.append(p)
        parent = os.path.dirname(d)
        if parent == d:
            return found
        d = parent


def build_key(repo, tree, grammar):
    """⟨TLS-34⟩ What a kept binary is keyed by: the source tree and the grammar
    clone's commit (the grammar is an unpinned clone outside git, review r1, sol).
    `None` when the build's inputs are not those two alone: a grammar clone given
    by a relative path (git and the build would resolve it from different folders),
    with local changes or untracked files (an added `scanner.c` is compiled), or
    with no commit; or build overrides (`build_overrides`). No binary is kept or
    seeded then."""
    if not os.path.isabs(grammar) or build_overrides(repo):
        return None
    try:
        rev = subprocess.run(["git", "-C", grammar, "rev-parse", "HEAD"], capture_output=True,
                             text=True, check=True).stdout.strip()
        dirty = subprocess.run(["git", "-C", grammar, "status", "--porcelain",
                                "--untracked-files=normal"], capture_output=True, text=True,
                               check=True).stdout.strip()
    except (OSError, subprocess.CalledProcessError):
        return None
    return None if dirty or not rev else f"{tree}-g{rev[:12]}"


def working_tree_key(repo):
    """The build key of the working tree when it is clean (no tracked change and
    no untracked file outside .gitignore, as ci-steps' gate-pass record asks);
    `None` otherwise."""
    if git(repo, "status", "--porcelain", "--untracked-files=normal"):
        return None
    return build_key(repo, git(repo, "rev-parse", "HEAD^{tree}"),
                     grammar_dir(repo, archive=False))


def record_tree_binary(repo, built, key_before):
    """⟨TLS-34⟩ Keep the working tree's release-fast binary in the cache under its
    build key, so `cached_binary` can seed a commit with that tree (and grammar)
    instead of building it. Only when the key taken before the build is the key
    after it (the sources did not change around the build), and the binary is
    copied into the cache before waiting for the cache lock (a later build into
    the same folder cannot swap it; review r1, sol). It differs in bytes from an
    archive build (the build folder's path is embedded), not in what it runs.
    Entries older than TREE_ENTRY_DAYS go (a branch that never lands).

    The binary is snapshotted first, as soon as cargo returns, before any check, and
    the caller holds `.cdo-speed-build.lock` in that folder from the first key through
    this snapshot, so no other cdo-speed builds there in between (review r2-r3, sol).
    Limit: ci-steps' own build and perf-bounds also use `<target>/gate-release`
    without that lock; a landing runs `ci-steps all` to its end before `cdo-gate`
    starts, so they do not overlap there.

    Keeping is an optimization: with no key it does nothing, and a file error only
    logs. It never fails the speed check, whose exit 1 means "slower" (review r1,
    Opus)."""
    if key_before is None:
        log("the work tree has changes, a modified grammar or build overrides: its "
            "binary is not kept for a later base")
        return
    try:
        _keep_tree_binary(repo, built, key_before)
    except (OSError, subprocess.CalledProcessError) as e:
        log(f"the working tree's binary was not kept (it only saves a later build): {e}")


def _keep_tree_binary(repo, built, key_before):
    root = cache_root()
    trees = os.path.join(root, "trees")
    os.makedirs(trees, exist_ok=True)
    # A unique name (review r1, Opus); a leftover of a killed run is pruned below.
    fd, snapshot = tempfile.mkstemp(dir=trees, prefix=".incoming-")
    os.close(fd)
    shutil.copy2(built, snapshot)
    key = working_tree_key(repo)
    if key != key_before:
        os.remove(snapshot)
        log("the sources changed during the build: its binary is not kept for a later base")
        return
    dst = os.path.join(trees, key, "alsem")
    with open(os.path.join(root, "lock"), "w") as lock:
        fcntl.flock(lock, fcntl.LOCK_EX)
        cutoff = time.time() - TREE_ENTRY_DAYS * 86400
        for name in os.listdir(trees):
            path = os.path.join(trees, name)
            if name.startswith(".incoming-"):
                # Another run's snapshot in flight is minutes old at most.
                if path != snapshot and os.path.getmtime(path) < time.time() - 86400:
                    os.remove(path)
            elif os.path.getmtime(path) < cutoff:
                shutil.rmtree(path, ignore_errors=True)
        if os.path.exists(dst):
            os.remove(snapshot)
        else:
            os.makedirs(os.path.dirname(dst), exist_ok=True)
            os.replace(snapshot, dst)
    log(f"kept the working tree's binary for {key[:10]}…{key[-13:]} (a later base of that "
        f"tree and grammar builds nothing)")


def cached_binary(repo, rev, logf, extra=()):
    """The release-fast alsem of commit `rev`, built once per commit and cached.
    ⟨TLS-61⟩ `extra` names more binaries of the same build (`aldump` for
    scripts/cdo-before-after), kept beside alsem in the same commit folder; only
    the missing ones are built."""
    sha = git(repo, "rev-parse", "--verify", f"{rev}^{{commit}}")
    root = cache_root()
    exe = os.path.join(root, "bin", sha, "alsem")
    os.makedirs(root, exist_ok=True)
    # One build at a time per cache (two gates may want the same commit).
    with open(os.path.join(root, "lock"), "w") as lock:
        fcntl.flock(lock, fcntl.LOCK_EX)
        missing = [b for b in ("alsem", *extra)
                   if not os.path.exists(os.path.join(os.path.dirname(exe), b))]
        if not missing:
            log(f"{rev} = {sha[:10]}: cached binary {exe}"
                + (f" (and {', '.join(extra)} beside it)" if extra else ""))
            return exe, sha
        # ⟨TLS-34⟩ A gate that timed this commit's exact tree left its binary
        # (`record_tree_binary`): a landing's merge commit has the tree its gate
        # tested, so the warm step after a landing builds nothing.
        key = build_key(repo, git(repo, "rev-parse", "--verify", f"{sha}^{{tree}}"),
                        grammar_dir(repo, archive=True))
        seeded = key and os.path.join(root, "trees", key, "alsem")
        if "alsem" in missing and seeded and os.path.exists(seeded):
            os.makedirs(os.path.dirname(exe), exist_ok=True)
            tmp = exe + ".tmp"
            shutil.copy2(seeded, tmp)
            os.replace(tmp, exe)
            log(f"{rev} = {sha[:10]}: seeded from a gate's build of its tree ({key[:10]}…) "
                f"(no build) at {exe}")
            missing.remove("alsem")
            if not missing:
                return exe, sha
        src = os.path.join(root, "build", "src")
        log(f"{rev} = {sha[:10]}: building {', '.join(missing)} (once per commit) in {src}")
        shutil.rmtree(src, ignore_errors=True)
        os.makedirs(src)
        # `tar -m`: every file gets the current time, so cargo rebuilds the
        # workspace crates. An archive keeps commit times, and an older commit's
        # files would look older than the last build's outputs (a stale binary).
        archive = subprocess.Popen(["git", "-C", repo, "archive", "--format=tar", sha],
                                   stdout=subprocess.PIPE)
        untar = subprocess.run(["tar", "-x", "-m", "-C", src], stdin=archive.stdout)
        archive.stdout.close()
        if archive.wait() != 0 or untar.returncode != 0:
            raise SystemExit(f"cdo-speed: git archive {sha} failed")
        env = dict(os.environ)
        env["CARGO_TARGET_DIR"] = os.path.join(root, "build", "target")
        if not env.get("TREE_SITTER_AL_PATH"):
            # The archive has no grammar clone; the checkout's own is the one
            # this repository builds against.
            # The same clone `build_key` reads for the seeding key (review r1, Opus).
            env["TREE_SITTER_AL_PATH"] = grammar_dir(repo, archive=True)
        t0 = time.monotonic()
        bins = [a for b in missing for a in ("--bin", b)]
        rc = subprocess.run(["cargo", "build", "--profile", "release-fast", *bins],
                            cwd=src, env=env, stdout=logf, stderr=subprocess.STDOUT).returncode
        if rc != 0:
            raise SystemExit(
                f"cdo-speed: building {rev} ({sha[:10]}) failed (log: {logf.name}). A grammar "
                "change since that commit stops its build (node-types.sha256); then pass a "
                "--base that builds against the current grammar, or skip the speed check.")
        os.makedirs(os.path.dirname(exe), exist_ok=True)
        for b in missing:
            dst = os.path.join(os.path.dirname(exe), b)
            tmp = dst + ".tmp"
            shutil.copy2(os.path.join(env["CARGO_TARGET_DIR"], "release-fast", b), tmp)
            os.replace(tmp, dst)
        log(f"  built in {time.monotonic() - t0:.0f} s")
        return exe, sha


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Measuring
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

def proc_status_kib(pid, key):
    try:
        with open(f"/proc/{pid}/status") as f:
            for line in f:
                if line.startswith(key + ":"):
                    return int(line.split()[1])
    except OSError:
        pass
    return 0


def proc_user_sys(pid):
    """(user, system) seconds of `pid` and of its children it has waited for."""
    try:
        with open(f"/proc/{pid}/stat") as f:
            stat = f.read()
        fields = stat[stat.rindex(")") + 2:].split()
        # utime, stime, cutime, cstime (fields 14-17 of proc(5)).
        utime, stime, cutime, cstime = (int(x) for x in fields[11:15])
        return (utime + cutime) / TICK, (stime + cstime) / TICK
    except (OSError, ValueError, IndexError):
        return 0.0, 0.0


def descendants(pid):
    """Every live descendant of `pid` (from /proc's parent links)."""
    parents = {}
    for d in os.listdir("/proc"):
        if not d.isdigit():
            continue
        try:
            with open(f"/proc/{d}/stat") as f:
                stat = f.read()
            parents.setdefault(int(stat[stat.rindex(")") + 2:].split()[1]), []).append(int(d))
        except (OSError, ValueError, IndexError):
            continue
    out, todo = [], [pid]
    while todo:
        kids = parents.get(todo.pop(), [])
        out.extend(kids)
        todo.extend(kids)
    return out


# ⟨TLS-83⟩ Instructions: user-mode instructions retired per call, next to its
# CPU seconds. On server1 (2026-11-18, one binary against itself, 3 runs) the
# model build's ratio held within about ±0.1 % (one 0.8 % jump in 12 builds;
# n = 6), against sd(log) 0.084 in CPU seconds: ~27x; judged calls ~40-400x.
# They do
# not see memory stalls, cache misses or lost parallelism, so CPU and wall keep
# judging; the instruction limit is ADVISORY (reported, never failing) until
# real landings show what it would catch (docs/superpowers/notes/
# 2026-11-18-tls83-speed-resolution.md). The events in use: both core types'
# counters on a hybrid CPU, else the plain one; empty when perf cannot count.
INSTR_EVENTS = ()
INSTR_WHY = ""
INSTR_LIMIT = 1.02
# How long a call waits for perf to attach (its `ack`) before it goes uncounted.
INSTR_ATTACH_S = float(os.environ.get("CDO_SPEED_INSTR_ATTACH_S", "5"))  # env: tests
# Failed attaches in a row after which a server's calls go uncounted.
INSTR_GIVE_UP = 3
# How long perf may take to stop after SIGINT; longer ends the server's counting.
INSTR_STOP_S = float(os.environ.get("CDO_SPEED_INSTR_STOP_S", "30"))  # env: tests
# ⟨TLS-111⟩ User cycles beside the instructions, per PMU: both are fixed counters
# (Intel's fixed counters 0 and 1), so the pair leaves nothing to multiplex.
HYBRID_EVENTS = ("cpu_core/instructions/u", "cpu_core/cycles/u",
                 "cpu_atom/instructions/u", "cpu_atom/cycles/u")
PLAIN_EVENTS = ("instructions:u", "cycles:u")
FIXED_KINDS = ("instructions", "cycles")
# CDO_SPEED_PERF: a test hook, the perf command to run (a stub in the tests).
PERF = os.environ.get("CDO_SPEED_PERF", "perf")


def event_pmu(event):
    """`cpu_core` of `cpu_core/cycles/u`; "" for a plain event (`cycles:u`)."""
    return event.split("/", 1)[0] if "/" in event else ""


def event_kind(event):
    """`cycles` of `cpu_core/cycles/u` or `cycles:u`."""
    return event.split("/")[1] if "/" in event else event.split(":", 1)[0]


def perf_event_args(events=None):
    """`-e` arguments for `events`: per PMU (`cpu_core/...`, `cpu_atom/...`, or
    plain), at most one `instructions` and one `cycles` event and nothing else.
    Both are fixed counters, so this perf run leaves nothing to multiplex: a
    count never comes out short because of our own events (review r1, sol;
    TLS-111 added cycles). Any other event, or one twice, is refused here.
    ⟨TLS-130⟩ A PMU's events go in one group (`-e {a,b}`): the kernel schedules a
    group's members together, so they run exactly as long. Apart, perf enables
    and reads them one after another, so a server thread running at the start
    or the stop of the count (more so under load) gives one of them more running
    time than the other, either one: on server1 (perf 7.0.14, 2026-11-25, 12
    spinning processes attached, no other perf user;
    /work/.al-sem/tools-notes/tls130/skew_probe.py, probe-2026-11-25.log) apart
    unequal in 10 of 10 stops (14 us to 9.2 ms), grouped 0 of 10; a gate under
    load refused counts that were whole.
    ⟨TLS-130 review r1⟩ Each group is PINNED (`:D`), which a group needs: its two
    members always tie, so time-slicing would not show in `perf_parse`. A pinned
    group is never time-sliced: it holds its counters whenever its tasks run, or
    it fails outright (another pinned user holds the counters) and counts
    nothing on that PMU. Measured (probe-pinned-2026-11-25.log): 16 ordinary
    extra cpu_core events, spinners on the P-cores: pinned 100.00 % running in 3
    of 3 stops, unpinned 49-50 %; attached (-p) beside a competing pinned group,
    ours read `<not counted>` at 0 ns and 100.00 % (enabled 0), which
    `perf_parse` refuses. What stays: see `covers`."""
    events = events or INSTR_EVENTS
    pairs = [(event_pmu(e), event_kind(e)) for e in events]
    if len(pairs) != len(set(pairs)) or any(k not in FIXED_KINDS for _, k in pairs):
        raise ValueError("cdo-speed: per PMU only `instructions` and `cycles`, once each "
                         f"(fixed counters, no multiplexing): {events}")
    pmus = dict.fromkeys(p for p, _ in pairs)
    return [a for pmu in pmus
            for a in ("-e", "{" + ",".join(e for e in events if event_pmu(e) == pmu) + "}:D")]


def perf_counts(text, events=None):
    """{event: count} of `perf_parse`, or None."""
    parsed = perf_parse(text, events)
    return parsed and parsed[0]


def perf_parse(text, events=None):
    """({event: count}, {event: running ns}, {event: running %}) from `perf
    stat -x,` output for `events`, or None. An event
    that never ran (a call that stayed on one core type) reads `<not counted>`:
    0. Any other non-number (`<not supported>`), or an event missing, is None.
    ⟨TLS-111⟩ Multiplexing is checked on the running time (perf's 4th column,
    exact nanoseconds): every event of one PMU must have run exactly as long. On
    server1 (perf 7.0.14, 2026-11-21) they did in all 20 attached runs of a
    process starting threads, and in every unforced run; with 16 extra events
    forcing multiplexing on cpu_core, its instructions ran 261.7 ms and its cycles
    241.0 ms. The running-% column (5th) is no check: it is running over a
    per-PMU enabled time, truncated to a whole percent, and the PMUs' shares of
    one kind summed to 110-125 % on real calls (and to 99 % on `true`), so a sum
    rule refused 9 of 64 calls in an A/A run on CDO (it now only withholds the
    CPU advisory, `partial_count`). Not caught here: a PMU whose events lose
    the same time (since TLS-130 they are one pinned group, which never
    time-slices; a group that fails reads `<not counted>`, see `partial_count`;
    the call's coverage, `covers`, is the other check). A `<not counted>` row runs 0 ns beside its sibling's 0; one at a
    non-zero running time, or a positive count at 0 ns, is no count (a count of
    0 at a non-zero time is real: `true` on an E-core for 0.17 ms). An event's
    row given twice is no count.
    ⟨TLS-130 review r1⟩ A `<not counted>` row must read 0.00 % running: a PMU the
    tasks never ran on was enabled for their time and ran 0 ns (0.00 %, on
    server1 and on real CDO calls). A pinned group that never got its counters
    has no enabled time either, and perf prints it at 100.00 %: no count."""
    events = events or INSTR_EVENTS
    counts, running, share = {}, {}, {}
    for line in text.splitlines():
        cols = line.strip().split(",")
        if len(cols) > 4 and cols[2] in events:
            if cols[2] in counts:
                return None
            if cols[0].isdigit():
                counts[cols[2]] = int(cols[0])
            elif cols[0] == "<not counted>":
                counts[cols[2]] = 0
            else:
                return None
            if not cols[3].isdigit():
                return None
            running[cols[2]] = int(cols[3])
            try:
                share[cols[2]] = float(cols[4])
            except ValueError:
                return None
            not_counted = cols[0] == "<not counted>"
            if (not_counted and (running[cols[2]] > 0 or cols[4] != "0.00")) or (
                    counts[cols[2]] > 0 and running[cols[2]] == 0):
                return None
    if set(counts) != set(events):
        return None
    for pmu in {event_pmu(e) for e in events}:
        if len({running[e] for e in events if event_pmu(e) == pmu}) != 1:
            return None
    return counts, running, share


def partial_count(parsed):
    """⟨TLS-130 review r2⟩ Why a parsed count (`perf_parse`) may be partial, or
    None when it is whole. Partial: a PMU's events read `<not counted>` (0 ns),
    or a kind's running % summed over the PMUs is under 100 (review r3, sol:
    no allowance). perf truncates each PMU's % to a whole number, so a whole
    count can read 99: `true` on server1 read 93.00 + 6.00 for 93.7 + 6.3
    (perf 7.0.14, 2026-11-21). Such a count is taken as partial too: that
    only withholds a CPU advisory, never hides a failure, while an allowance
    let a really partial 80 + 19 count waive one. A pinned group that fails after its
    tasks ran on that PMU reads exactly like a PMU they never ran on, so a
    `<not counted>` PMU is always taken as partial. A partial count still gives
    an instruction count (an advisory ratio), but never a CPU advisory
    (`cpu_advisory`). Two sides whose counted PMUs differ are covered too: one
    of them has a `<not counted>` PMU."""
    counts, running, share = parsed
    lost = sorted({event_pmu(e) for e in counts if running[e] == 0})
    if lost:
        return f"{', '.join(p or 'cpu' for p in lost)} not counted"
    for kind in FIXED_KINDS:
        total = sum(s for e, s in share.items() if event_kind(e) == kind)
        if total < 100:
            return f"{kind} ran {total:.2f} % of the time summed over the PMUs"
    return None


def kind_total(counts, kind):
    """The summed count of `kind` over the PMUs, or None (no count, or no event
    of that kind)."""
    if counts is None:
        return None
    values = [n for e, n in counts.items() if event_kind(e) == kind]
    return sum(values) if values else None


def instr_total(text, events=None):
    """The summed user-mode instructions of `perf stat -x,` output, or None."""
    return kind_total(perf_counts(text, events), "instructions")


def instr_probe():
    """(events, why): the events that count user-mode instructions and cycles
    here, or ((), the reason none can). CDO_SPEED_INSTR=0 turns counting off."""
    if os.environ.get("CDO_SPEED_INSTR") == "0":
        return (), "off (CDO_SPEED_INSTR=0)"
    why = "perf gave no count"
    for events in (HYBRID_EVENTS, PLAIN_EVENTS):
        try:
            r = subprocess.run([PERF, "stat", "--no-scale", "-x,", *perf_event_args(events),
                                "--", "true"], capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=30)
        except (OSError, subprocess.TimeoutExpired) as e:
            return (), f"perf did not run ({e})"
        if r.returncode == 0 and instr_total(r.stderr, events):
            return events, ""
        why = (r.stderr.strip().splitlines() or [f"exit {r.returncode}"])[-1]
    return (), f"perf cannot count user-mode instructions here ({why}); scripts/perf-probe"


class Server:
    """One `alsem mcp` server over stdio (newline-delimited JSON-RPC)."""

    def __init__(self, exe, ws, outdir, name, env=None):
        self.name = name
        self.root = tempfile.mkdtemp(prefix=f"cdo-speed-{name}-", dir=outdir)
        self.err = open(os.path.join(outdir, f"{name}.server.err"), "wb")
        self.p = subprocess.Popen(
            [exe, "mcp", "--workspace", ws, "--output-root", self.root],
            stdin=subprocess.PIPE, stdout=subprocess.PIPE, stderr=self.err,
            env=child_env(env))
        self.buf = b""
        self.next_id = 0
        # pid -> the highest VmHWM read for it (kept after the process exits).
        self.hwm_kib = {}
        r = self.request("initialize", {"protocolVersion": "2025-06-18", "capabilities": {},
                                        "clientInfo": {"name": "cdo-speed", "version": "1"}})
        if "result" not in r:
            raise SystemExit(f"cdo-speed: {name}: initialize failed: {r}")
        self.send({"jsonrpc": "2.0", "method": "notifications/initialized", "params": {}})

    def send(self, msg):
        self.p.stdin.write((json.dumps(msg) + "\n").encode())
        self.p.stdin.flush()

    def readline(self, deadline):
        while b"\n" not in self.buf:
            left = deadline - time.monotonic()
            if left <= 0:
                raise SystemExit(f"cdo-speed: {self.name}: an MCP call timed out")
            ready, _, _ = select.select([self.p.stdout], [], [], left)
            if ready:
                chunk = os.read(self.p.stdout.fileno(), 1 << 20)
                if not chunk:
                    raise SystemExit(f"cdo-speed: {self.name}: the MCP server closed its stdout")
                self.buf += chunk
        line, self.buf = self.buf.split(b"\n", 1)
        return json.loads(line)

    def request(self, method, params):
        self.next_id += 1
        rid = self.next_id
        self.send({"jsonrpc": "2.0", "id": rid, "method": method, "params": params})
        deadline = time.monotonic() + CALL_TIMEOUT
        while True:
            msg = self.readline(deadline)
            if msg.get("id") == rid:
                return msg

    def user_sys(self):
        """(user, system) seconds of the server and every live engine child
        (with their own waited-for children)."""
        pairs = [proc_user_sys(p) for p in [self.p.pid, *descendants(self.p.pid)]]
        return sum(u for u, _ in pairs), sum(s for _, s in pairs)

    def call(self, tool, args):
        """(wall s, CPU s, stdout sha256, exit code, first stderr lines) of one
        tool call; exit code -1 when the engine failed (no CLI exit).
        `self.last_instr` then holds the call's user-mode instructions, or None
        when they are not counted (TLS-83); `self.last_counts` every counted
        event's count ({event: n}, or None), and `self.last_user_sys` its user
        and system seconds (TLS-111). `self.last_refusal` says why a counting
        run gave this call no count, else None (TLS-130 review r1: `timed_call`
        puts it in the call's record, so a reader ties it to that call)."""
        self.last_refusal = None
        perf = self.instr_start()
        # instr_stop runs even when the call times out (SystemExit): perf and
        # its FIFOs never outlive the call (review r1, sol).
        try:
            u0, s0 = self.user_sys()
            t0 = time.monotonic()
            r = self.request("tools/call", {"name": tool, "arguments": args})
            wall = time.monotonic() - t0
            u1, s1 = self.user_sys()
            self.last_user_sys = (u1 - u0, s1 - s0)
            cpu = (u1 - u0) + (s1 - s0)
            if TEST_WALL_IS_CPU:
                # Plus the wall-only seconds a stub declares (TLS-111 review r5).
                stub = r.get("result", {}).get("structuredContent", {})
                wall = cpu + float(stub.get("stubSleptS", 0) or 0)
        finally:
            parsed = self.instr_stop(perf)
            self.last_counts = parsed and parsed[0]
            self.last_instr = kind_total(self.last_counts, "instructions")
            # ⟨TLS-111⟩ The instructions events' running time over the PMUs:
            # the counters' coverage of the call (`covers`).
            self.last_running_s = parsed and kind_total(parsed[1], "instructions") / 1e9
            # ⟨TLS-130 review r2⟩ Why the count may be partial (no CPU advisory).
            self.last_partial = parsed and partial_count(parsed)
        self.sample_hwm()
        if "error" in r:
            raise SystemExit(f"cdo-speed: {self.name}: {tool}: {r['error']}")
        env = r["result"].get("structuredContent", {})
        if "stdoutFile" in env:
            sha = env["stdoutFile"].get("sha256", "")[:16]
        else:
            text = env.get("stdoutText", env.get("output", ""))
            if not isinstance(text, str):
                text = json.dumps(text, sort_keys=True)
            sha = hashlib.sha256(text.encode()).hexdigest()[:16]
        err = env.get("stderr") or [env.get("message", "")]
        if isinstance(err, str):
            err = err.splitlines()
        return wall, cpu, sha, env.get("exitCode", -1), [str(x) for x in err if x][:5]

    # ⟨TLS-83⟩ User-mode instructions over a call (INSTR_EVENTS, chosen at the
    # start by instr_probe), from `perf stat` attached to the server and its live
    # children; threads and children they start during the call are counted too
    # (inherit). Unscaled (`--no-scale`): an unpinned thread moves between core
    # types, and perf's default scaling would inflate each type's partial count.
    def instr_start(self):
        # After INSTR_GIVE_UP attaches in a row fail, this server counts no more:
        # each failure costs up to INSTR_ATTACH_S inside the CDO slot, 66 calls a
        # run (review r1, sol).
        if not INSTR_EVENTS:
            return None
        if getattr(self, "instr_failures", 0) >= INSTR_GIVE_UP:
            # No line per call: the server-level line below said it once.
            self.last_refusal = "this server's perf gave up"
            return None
        started = self._instr_start()
        self.instr_failures = 0 if started else getattr(self, "instr_failures", 0) + 1
        if not started:
            cause = getattr(self, "attach_error", "")
            self.last_refusal = f"perf attach failed: {cause}"
            # One line per failed attach (at most INSTR_GIVE_UP a server): what the
            # tests count, since a stub perf too slow to start logs nothing (TLS-103).
            print(f"cdo-speed: {self.name} (pid {self.p.pid}): perf attach failed "
                  f"({self.instr_failures}/{INSTR_GIVE_UP}): {cause}", file=sys.stderr)
        if self.instr_failures == INSTR_GIVE_UP:
            print(f"cdo-speed: {self.name}: perf failed to attach {INSTR_GIVE_UP} times in a row; "
                  "this server's calls are not counted from here on", file=sys.stderr)
        return started

    def _instr_start(self):
        # Counting must never fail a run (review r1, Fable: a fork that fails
        # while the gate holds 16-28 GB, or a perf gone since the probe): the
        # call is then just not counted.
        # The call starts only once perf says its counters run: perf starts them
        # disabled (`-D -1`), we write `enable` to its control FIFO and wait for
        # its `ack` (at most INSTR_ATTACH_S). A fixed sleep raced a slow perf start
        # under load and counted a call from part-way through (TLS-92's gate: a
        # 0-count call). No ack in time: the call is not counted.
        perf = ctl_fd = ack_fd = None
        try:
            attached = [self.p.pid, *descendants(self.p.pid)]
            pids = ",".join(str(p) for p in attached)
            work = tempfile.mkdtemp(prefix="perf-", dir=self.root)
            out, ctl, ack = (os.path.join(work, n) for n in ("out.csv", "ctl", "ack"))
            os.mkfifo(ctl)
            os.mkfifo(ack)
            ack_fd = os.open(ack, os.O_RDONLY | os.O_NONBLOCK)
            perf = subprocess.Popen([PERF, "stat", "--no-scale", "-x,", *perf_event_args(),
                                     "-D", "-1", "--control", f"fifo:{ctl},{ack}",
                                     "-o", out, "-p", pids],
                                    stdout=subprocess.DEVNULL, stderr=subprocess.DEVNULL)
            deadline = time.monotonic() + INSTR_ATTACH_S
            # perf opens its end while parsing its options, BEFORE its counters
            # exist: this wait does not mean they run. Only the `ack` below does
            # (review r1, Fable; TLS-83's proof: without the ack wait, red).
            while ctl_fd is None:
                try:
                    ctl_fd = os.open(ctl, os.O_WRONLY | os.O_NONBLOCK)
                except OSError:  # ENXIO: no reader yet
                    # A named cause, not ENXIO's text (TLS-130 review r3, Fable).
                    if perf.poll() is not None:
                        raise OSError("perf ended before opening its control FIFO")
                    if time.monotonic() > deadline:
                        raise OSError("perf did not open its control FIFO in time")
                    time.sleep(0.005)
            os.write(ctl_fd, b"enable\n")
            got = b""
            while b"ack" not in got:
                left = deadline - time.monotonic()
                if left <= 0 or not select.select([ack_fd], [], [], left)[0]:
                    raise OSError("perf gave no ack")
                chunk = os.read(ack_fd, 64)
                if not chunk and perf.poll() is not None:
                    raise OSError("perf ended before its ack")
                got += chunk
            # A process that appeared between the pid scan and the attach (an
            # engine child restarted) is not counted, inherit or not: such a
            # call's count would be partial (review r1, sol).
            if set(descendants(self.p.pid)) - set(attached):
                raise OSError("a server process started before perf attached")
        except OSError as e:
            # ⟨TLS-130 review r2, Fable⟩ The cause, for the call's reason: no
            # ack, perf ending first, a server process started first, or the
            # OS error itself.
            self.attach_error = str(e) or type(e).__name__
            for fd in (ctl_fd, ack_fd):
                if fd is not None:
                    os.close(fd)
            if perf is not None:
                perf.kill()
                try:
                    perf.wait(timeout=10)
                except subprocess.TimeoutExpired:
                    pass
            return None
        return perf, out, (ctl_fd, ack_fd)

    def instr_stop(self, started):
        """The call's `perf_parse` ({event: n}, {event: running ns}), or None when
        perf could not give them (the call then has no instruction ratio; CPU and
        wall still judge it)."""
        if started is None:
            return None
        perf, out, fds = started
        # A perf that ended before we stopped it counted only part of the call
        # (its output can still be valid CSV): no count (review r2, sol).
        early = perf.poll() is not None
        if not early:
            perf.send_signal(signal.SIGINT)
        try:
            perf.wait(timeout=INSTR_STOP_S)
        except subprocess.TimeoutExpired:
            perf.kill()
            try:
                perf.wait(timeout=10)
            except subprocess.TimeoutExpired:
                pass
            total = None
            # A perf that hangs at its stop would cost 30 s on every call: this
            # server counts no more (review r2, sol; an ack resets the attach
            # streak, so the attach give-up alone did not catch it).
            self.instr_failures = INSTR_GIVE_UP
            self.last_refusal = "perf did not stop"
            print(f"cdo-speed: {self.name}: perf did not stop within {INSTR_STOP_S:g} s; "
                  "this server's calls are not counted from here on", file=sys.stderr)
        else:
            if early:
                total = None
                self.last_refusal = "perf ended before the call did"
                print(f"cdo-speed: {self.name}: perf ended before the call did; call not counted",
                      file=sys.stderr)
            else:
                try:
                    with open(out) as f:
                        text = f.read()
                    total = perf_parse(text)
                    if total is None:
                        # Why a call goes uncounted (multiplexing, a malformed
                        # row): perf's own rows, for the log.
                        rows = [ln.strip() for ln in text.splitlines()
                                if ln.strip() and not ln.startswith("#")]
                        self.last_refusal = "perf output not taken as a count"
                        print(f"cdo-speed: {self.name}: perf output not taken as a count: "
                              f"{' | '.join(rows[:6]) or '(empty)'}", file=sys.stderr)
                except OSError as e:
                    total = None
                    self.last_refusal = "perf output unreadable"
                    print(f"cdo-speed: {self.name}: perf output unreadable ({e}); "
                          "call not counted", file=sys.stderr)
        for fd in fds:
            os.close(fd)
        shutil.rmtree(os.path.dirname(out), ignore_errors=True)
        # No instruction at all (every event `<not counted>`) is no count: as a 0
        # it would pull the round total down (review r1, Fable).
        if total and not kind_total(total[0], "instructions"):
            print(f"cdo-speed: {self.name}: perf counted no instruction; call not counted",
                  file=sys.stderr)
            self.last_refusal = "perf counted no instruction"
            total = None
        return total

    def sample_hwm(self):
        """Read the VmHWM of the server and every live child. Called after each
        call, so a child that exits later in the run still counts."""
        for p in [self.p.pid, *descendants(self.p.pid)]:
            kib = proc_status_kib(p, "VmHWM")
            if kib > self.hwm_kib.get(p, 0):
                self.hwm_kib[p] = kib

    def peak_mib(self):
        """Every server process's peak RSS (VmHWM), summed, including the
        processes that exited during the run."""
        self.sample_hwm()
        return sum(self.hwm_kib.values()) / 1024

    def close(self):
        # ⟨TLS-79⟩ The whole tree must be gone: an engine child left behind keeps
        # its model resident, which is what made a build beside it slower.
        kids = descendants(self.p.pid)
        try:
            self.p.stdin.close()
            self.p.wait(timeout=60)
        except (OSError, subprocess.TimeoutExpired):
            self.p.kill()
        deadline = time.monotonic() + 30
        left = [k for k in kids if os.path.exists(f"/proc/{k}")]
        while left and time.monotonic() < deadline:
            time.sleep(0.2)
            left = [k for k in left if os.path.exists(f"/proc/{k}")]
        for k in left:
            log(f"server {self.name}: child {k} still alive 30 s after the close: killed")
            try:
                os.kill(k, 9)
            except OSError:
                pass
        deadline = time.monotonic() + 5
        while left and time.monotonic() < deadline:
            time.sleep(0.1)
            left = [k for k in left if os.path.exists(f"/proc/{k}")]
        for k in left:
            log(f"server {self.name}: child {k} survived SIGKILL: its memory may still be "
                f"resident (the next build's timing may be skewed)")
        self.err.close()
        shutil.rmtree(self.root, ignore_errors=True)


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The run
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

class CallFailed(Exception):
    """A timed call that did not exit 0: (what, its first stderr lines)."""


def timed_call(server, side, cid, tool, args, rnd, warm_pass, tag, records):
    """One timed call on `side`'s server, logged and recorded."""
    load = load_1min()
    pressure, _ = cpu_pressure()
    what = f"{tag} {cid} {side}"
    before = SUSPENDS.note(f"before {what}") if SUSPENDS else 0
    # Read again even when the call fails or times out (review, sol): the
    # verdict then still says the host slept.
    try:
        wall, cpu, sha, code, err = server.call(tool, args)
    finally:
        during = SUSPENDS.note(f"during {what}") if SUSPENDS else 0
    exit_note = f"  exit {code}" if code != 0 else ""
    shown = "?" if pressure is None else f"{pressure:.1f}"
    instr = getattr(server, "last_instr", None)
    counts = getattr(server, "last_counts", None)
    cycles = kind_total(counts, "cycles")
    user, system = getattr(server, "last_user_sys", (None, None))
    running_s = getattr(server, "last_running_s", None)
    covered = covers(running_s, cpu) if instr else None
    instr_note = "" if instr is None else f"  instr {instr / 1e9:8.3f}e9"
    if cycles:
        instr_note += f"  cycles {cycles / 1e9:8.3f}e9"
    if covered is False:
        instr_note += f"  NOT COVERED (counters ran {running_s:.2f} s)"
    log(f"  {tag} {cid:<12} {side:<5} wall {wall:7.2f} s  cpu {cpu:7.2f} s"
        f"  load {load:5.1f}  pressure {shown:>5}{instr_note}{exit_note}")
    if code != 0:
        raise CallFailed(f"{tag} {cid} ({tool}) on {side} exited {code}", err)
    # ⟨TLS-130⟩ An uncovered count is partial (a multiplexed group loses the same
    # time on both members, so only `covers` sees it): no instruction count; its
    # counts stay for diagnostics.
    # ⟨TLS-130 review r1⟩ `uncounted`: why this call has no count (None when it
    # has one, or when the run counts nothing), tied to its own record.
    refusal = getattr(server, "last_refusal", None)
    if covered is False:
        instr = None
        refusal = "not covered"
    # ⟨TLS-130 review r2⟩ `partial`: why this call's count may be partial
    # (`partial_count`); such a call never makes a CPU failure advisory.
    partial = getattr(server, "last_partial", None) if instr else None
    records.append(dict(call=cid, side=side, round=rnd, warm_pass=warm_pass,
                        wall=wall, cpu=cpu, sha=sha, load=load, pressure=pressure,
                        instr=instr, suspended_before=before, suspended_during=during,
                        user=user, sys=system, cycles=cycles, counts=counts,
                        running_s=running_s, covered=covered, uncounted=refusal,
                        partial=partial, **clock_cpi(cycles, user, instr, covered)))


# ⟨TLS-111 review r2, sol⟩ Coverage: equal running times on a PMU's events do not
# show that the counters ran for the whole call (both could miss the same
# interval). The instructions events' running time, summed over the PMUs, is
# compared with the call's CPU seconds from /proc (user + system). A coverage
# loss can only make the running time SHORTER, so the check is one-sided: the
# count covers the call unless running < CPU x (1 - COVER_REL) - COVER_ABS
# (/proc's 10 ms ticks, read at both ends, over several processes). Measured:
# - synthetic calls on server1 (2026-11-21, perf 7.0.14, cdo-speed's own Server
#   and attach; 40 calls of 0-12 s CPU in threads and child processes forked
#   during the call; /work/.al-sem/tools-notes/tls111/coverage-probe.log): at
#   most 0.6 % apart on 12 s, 0.021 s below 3 s, one 0.009 s tick on 0.05 s;
# - CDO, an A/A run of f7da9a93 (/work/.al-sem/tools-notes/tls111/aa3): on the
#   39 calls above 1 s, running minus CPU was -0.5 % to +2.7 %, and every build
#   and warm-tests call (the multi-threaded ones) read +2.2 to +2.7 % (+2.23 to
#   +2.66 %; build 184.41 s running against 179.63 s CPU); calls at or below 1 s
#   were within -25 to +22 ms. The excess is probably /proc's tick sampling
#   against perf's exact times on many threads: a guess, not a measured cause. A
#   two-sided 2 % band refused all 8 of those calls.
# The one-sided band's cost: on a multi-threaded call that reads about +2.5 %, a
# real coverage loss of up to about 4.7 % still passes. Near the limits that can
# flip an advisory (TLS-130 review r1, sol), so a CPU advisory also needs both
# sides' counts whole (`partial_count`).
# A running time above CPU x COVER_CAP + COVER_ABS is no loss but is odd (perf
# counting something /proc does not): no count either. The cap is x1.10, about
# four times the largest excess seen on CDO. An uncovered count gives no
# instruction count (TLS-130), so no clock, label or advisory; its counts stay
# in the record.
# ⟨TLS-130⟩ The one partial count left: a pinned group (`perf_event_args`) is
# never time-sliced, and one that never got its counters is refused by
# `perf_parse` (100.00 %). One that fails AFTER its tasks first ran on that PMU
# reads like a PMU they never ran on (`<not counted>`, 0 ns, 0.00 %): perf's CSV
# has no enabled time to tell them apart, and perf -v's enabled times differ
# between the PMUs even when nothing fails (1.93 s against 1.29 s on a process
# starting threads and children; enabled_probe.py). Coverage misses it when
# that PMU carried less of the call's CPU seconds than this band (2 % + 0.05
# s): the count then stays an instruction count, but `partial_count` takes
# every `<not counted>` PMU as partial, so it never gives a CPU advisory.
# A second shape, not reproduced (it needs a CPU-wide pinned rival): a thread or
# child started during the call (inherited) whose pinned group fails while the
# parent's holds is expected to read as a normal row with a shorter running
# time, not `<not counted>`; again only coverage catches it, same band. A loss
# under the band is not seen and can still make a CPU failure advisory
# (TLS-130 review r3, Fable).
COVER_REL = 0.02
COVER_ABS = 0.05
COVER_CAP = 1.10


def covers(running_s, cpu):
    """Whether the counters' running time covers the call's CPU seconds: not
    short of it beyond COVER_REL + COVER_ABS, nor over COVER_CAP of it."""
    return (running_s is not None
            and cpu * (1 - COVER_REL) - COVER_ABS <= running_s <= cpu * COVER_CAP + COVER_ABS)


def clock_cpi(cycles, user, instr, covered=True):
    """⟨TLS-111⟩ The call's clock (user cycles / user seconds, GHz) and cycles
    per instruction, each None when a part is missing or zero, or when the
    counts do not cover the call (`covers`): such a count stays in the record
    for diagnostics, but gives no CLOCK or CPI label, so it never makes a CPU
    failure advisory."""
    if not covered:
        return dict(clock=None, cpi=None)
    return dict(clock=cycles / user / 1e9 if cycles and user else None,
                cpi=cycles / instr if cycles and instr else None)


def run_round(rnd, servers, records, warm_pass=0, skip=0):
    """One round's calls (`skip`: leave out the first calls of the warm-up, done
    already)."""
    order = ["base", "head"] if rnd % 2 == 0 else ["head", "base"]
    if rnd == 0:
        calls = WARM_UP
    else:
        calls = calls_for(ROUND_ROUTINES[rnd - 1])
        log(f"round {rnd}: {ROUND_ROUTINES[rnd - 1]}")
    for i, (cid, tool, args) in enumerate(calls):
        if i < skip:
            continue
        # The warm-up flips the order per call, and its second pass (fresh
        # servers) flips every call again: each warm-up call is timed once with
        # each side second (measured: going second costs up to ~15 % under load).
        side_order = order if rnd != 0 or i % 2 == 0 else order[::-1]
        if rnd == 0 and warm_pass % 2 == 1:
            side_order = side_order[::-1]
        tag = f"r0.{warm_pass + 1}" if rnd == 0 else f"r{rnd}"
        for side in side_order:
            timed_call(servers[side], side, cid, tool, args, rnd, warm_pass, tag, records)


def judge(records):
    """Per call: (rows, the calls too noisy to judge)."""
    rows, noisy = [], []
    calls = list(dict.fromkeys(r["call"] for r in records))
    rounds = sorted({r["round"] for r in records if r["round"] > 0})
    # The round total: every counted call's time summed per round and side.
    for side in ("base", "head"):
        for rnd in rounds:
            rs = [r for r in records if r["round"] == rnd and r["side"] == side]

            def summed(key):
                values = [r.get(key) for r in rs]
                return sum(values) if values and None not in values else None
            instr, cycles, user = summed("instr"), summed("cycles"), summed("user")
            covered = bool(rs) and all(r.get("covered") for r in rs)
            records.append(dict(call="round-total", side=side, round=rnd, total=True,
                                wall=sum(r["wall"] for r in rs),
                                cpu=sum(r["cpu"] for r in rs), sha="",
                                instr=instr, cycles=cycles, user=user, sys=summed("sys"),
                                running_s=summed("running_s"),
                                partial="; ".join(f"{r['call']}: {r['partial']}" for r in rs
                                                  if r.get("partial")) or None,
                                covered=covered, **clock_cpi(cycles, user, instr, covered)))
    calls.append("round-total")
    for cid in calls:
        rs = [r for r in records if r["call"] == cid]
        a = {r["round"]: r for r in rs if r["side"] == "base"}
        b = {r["round"]: r for r in rs if r["side"] == "head"}
        paired = sorted(set(a) & set(b))
        row = dict(call=cid, rounds=paired, same_output=(
            [a[k]["sha"] for k in paired] == [b[k]["sha"] for k in paired]))
        # ⟨TLS-83⟩ Instructions (and TLS-111's clock and cycles per instruction)
        # only where every paired call has a value.
        counted = [m for m in ("instr", "clock", "cpi") if paired and all(
            (a[k].get(m) or 0) > 0 and (b[k].get(m) or 0) > 0 for k in paired)]
        for m in ["cpu", "wall", *counted]:
            ratios = [b[k][m] / a[k][m] if a[k][m] > 0 else 1.0 for k in paired]
            row[m] = dict(base=statistics.median(a[k][m] for k in paired),
                          head=statistics.median(b[k][m] for k in paired),
                          ratio=statistics.median(ratios), ratios=ratios,
                          spread=(max(ratios) / min(ratios)) if min(ratios) > 0 else 9.9)
        # ⟨TLS-111 review r4, sol⟩ The CPU ratio at equal clocks, per pair: the
        # clock is user cycles per USER second, so only head's user time is
        # scaled by the clock ratio (head/base); its system time is not:
        # (head user x clock ratio + head sys) / (base user + base sys).
        if "clock" in counted and all(
                r.get("user") is not None and r.get("sys") is not None and r["cpu"] > 0
                for k in paired for r in (a[k], b[k])):
            ratios = [(b[k]["user"] * b[k]["clock"] / a[k]["clock"] + b[k]["sys"])
                      / (a[k]["user"] + a[k]["sys"]) for k in paired]
            row["corrected"] = dict(ratio=statistics.median(ratios), ratios=ratios)
        # ⟨TLS-130 reviews r2 and r3⟩ Why a paired count may be partial, per pair
        # (`partial_rounds`, in `paired` order; `cpu_advisory` reads only the
        # pairs it judges): `partial_count`'s reason, or a count not shown to
        # cover its call (`covers`; a counted call always covers, so this
        # restates `timed_call`'s rule where `cpu_advisory` reads it). The row
        # then gets no CPU advisory, and its failure says why.
        def why(r):
            if r.get("partial"):
                return r["partial"]
            return None if r.get("covered") else "count not shown to cover the call"
        row["partial_rounds"] = ["; ".join(f"{side} {r['call']} r{k}: {why(r)}"
                                           for side, r in (("base", a[k]), ("head", b[k]))
                                           if why(r)) or None
                                 for k in paired]
        row["partial"] = "; ".join(p for p in row["partial_rounds"] if p) or None
        row["judged"] = row["cpu"]["base"] >= MIN_SECONDS
        rows.append(row)
        # Undecided: the rounds disagree about the verdict (some ratios over
        # the limit, some not). The ratios differ by routine on a real change
        # too (round 4's slowdown cost each routine its own number of
        # searches), so a wide spread alone is not noise.
        if row["judged"] and any(
                min(row[m]["ratios"]) <= lim < max(row[m]["ratios"])
                for m, lim in (("cpu", CPU_LIMIT), ("wall", WALL_LIMIT))):
            noisy.append(cid)
    return rows, noisy


def ir_build_rows(exes, ws, outdir, ir_cold):
    """⟨ENG-110⟩ The reported (never judged) IR cache rows, after the judged timing: per
    side, an untimed fill of a fresh IR cache folder, then a `build (IR warm)` on it; with
    `ir_cold`, also a `build (IR cold)` on another fresh folder. Each side's engine report is
    checked with `ir-cache-report-check --rule warm-build`; a breach labels the verdict and
    never fails it. A base without a report predates the cache: said, not a breach.
    Returns the label."""
    _, b_tool, b_args = WARM_UP[0]
    checker = os.path.join(os.path.dirname(os.path.abspath(__file__)), "ir-cache-report-check")
    breaches, times = [], {}

    def build(side, kind, root):
        rep = os.path.join(outdir, f"ir-{kind}-{side}.jsonl")
        env = {"ALSEM_IR_CACHE": "on", "ALSEM_IR_CACHE_DIR": root, "ALSEM_IR_CACHE_REPORT": rep}
        s = Server(exes[side], ws, outdir, f"{side}-ir-{kind}", env)
        try:
            wall, cpu, _, code, err = s.call(b_tool, b_args)
        finally:
            s.close()
        if code != 0:
            raise CallFailed(f"build (IR {kind}) on {side} exited {code}", err)
        return rep, wall, cpu

    for side in ("base", "head"):
        root = tempfile.mkdtemp(prefix=f"ir-{side}-", dir=outdir)
        build(side, "fill", root)
        rep, wall, cpu = build(side, "warm", root)
        times[("warm", side)] = (wall, cpu)
        if not os.path.exists(rep) and side == "base":
            log(f"  build (IR warm): base wrote no IR cache report (it predates ENG-110)")
        else:
            r = subprocess.run([checker, "--rule", "warm-build", rep],
                               capture_output=True, text=True)
            if r.returncode != 0:
                breaches.append(f"{side}: {r.stdout.strip()}")
        if ir_cold:
            cold = tempfile.mkdtemp(prefix=f"ir-cold-{side}-", dir=outdir)
            _, wall, cpu = build(side, "cold", cold)
            times[("cold", side)] = (wall, cpu)
    for kind in ("warm", "cold"):
        if (kind, "base") not in times:
            continue
        (bw, bc), (hw, hc) = times[(kind, "base")], times[(kind, "head")]
        log(f"  {'build (IR ' + kind + ')':<14} cpu {bc:7.2f} -> {hc:7.2f} s x{hc / bc:.3f}"
            f"   wall {bw:7.2f} -> {hw:7.2f} s x{hw / bw:.3f}  (reported only)")
    return f" IR WARM REPORT BREACH ({'; '.join(breaches)})" if breaches else ""


def main():
    ap = argparse.ArgumentParser(description=__doc__,
                                 formatter_class=argparse.RawDescriptionHelpFormatter)
    ap.add_argument("workspace", nargs="?")
    ap.add_argument("--warm", metavar="REV",
                    help="only build REV's binary into the cache (as a gate would build its base) "
                         "and exit; no workspace, no timing (TLS-18: run after a landing)")
    ap.add_argument("--base", help="the base commit (default: merge-base HEAD master)")
    ap.add_argument("--head", help="a commit to time instead of the working tree")
    ap.add_argument("--base-exe", help="a built base binary (noise measurements; no build)")
    ap.add_argument("--head-exe", help="a built head binary (noise measurements; no build)")
    ap.add_argument("--rounds", type=int, default=3,
                    help=f"timed rounds, 1 to {len(ROUND_ROUTINES)} (one new routine per round; "
                         "default 3)")
    ap.add_argument("--max-rounds", type=int, default=len(ROUND_ROUTINES))
    ap.add_argument("--out", help="the folder for outputs and speed.json")
    ap.add_argument("--build-only", action="store_true", help="build both binaries, time nothing")
    ap.add_argument("--ir-cold", action="store_true",
                    help="ENG-110: also report a model build on a fresh IR cache folder per side "
                         "(reported only)")
    ap.add_argument("--no-build", action="store_true",
                    help="the working tree's release-fast alsem is already built "
                         "(in <target>/gate-release, where --build-only builds it)")
    ap.add_argument("--warm-samples", type=int, default=WARM_SAMPLES, choices=[1, 2],
                    help="warm-up passes on fresh servers, the second flipped (default 2; "
                         "1 = one pass, base built first: a quick look, biased under load)")
    args = ap.parse_args()
    # TLS-25: each round times a routine no round timed before, so there are at most as many
    # rounds as routines. Refused here, before anything is built or started: a run asked for
    # more once timed the warm-up and every round (minutes on CDO), then stopped with an
    # IndexError and wrote no speed.json.
    if not 1 <= args.rounds <= len(ROUND_ROUTINES):
        ap.error(f"--rounds {args.rounds}: give 1 to {len(ROUND_ROUTINES)}, one round per "
                 f"routine in ROUND_ROUTINES (a routine timed again measures nothing new)")
    args.max_rounds = min(max(args.max_rounds, args.rounds), len(ROUND_ROUTINES))
    # TLS-103 review (sol): the stub clock never judges a real run. It is honoured
    # only as `1` and with both prebuilt binaries given (the stub tests' shape; a gate
    # builds its own and gives neither), so a variable left exported in a lane shell
    # cannot make a real gate pass on CPU alone.
    hook = os.environ.get("CDO_SPEED_TEST_WALL_IS_CPU")
    if hook is not None and (hook != "1" or not (args.base_exe and args.head_exe)):
        ap.error("CDO_SPEED_TEST_WALL_IS_CPU is a test hook for stub servers: set it to 1, "
                 "and only with --base-exe and --head-exe (unset it for a real run)")

    if not sys.platform.startswith("linux"):
        log("this gate reads /proc: Linux only")
        return 2
    # ⟨ENG-110⟩ VERIFY parses every hit again: never in a timed run.
    if os.environ.get("ALSEM_IR_CACHE_VERIFY"):
        log("ALSEM_IR_CACHE_VERIFY is set: a timed run never verifies (unset it)")
        return 2
    repo = os.path.dirname(os.path.dirname(os.path.abspath(__file__)))
    if args.warm:
        # The gate's own builder and cache key, so the next gate finds exactly the binary it
        # would have built (and waits on the cache lock if this build is still running).
        os.makedirs(cache_root(), exist_ok=True)
        with open(os.path.join(cache_root(), "warm.log"), "a") as logf:
            exe, sha = cached_binary(repo, args.warm, logf)
        log(f"warm: {args.warm} = {sha[:10]} cached at {exe}")
        return 0
    if not args.workspace:
        log("a workspace is required (or --warm REV)")
        return 2
    ws = os.path.abspath(args.workspace)
    if not os.path.isdir(ws):
        log(f"no workspace at {ws}")
        return 2
    outdir = os.path.abspath(args.out or os.path.join(
        target_dir(repo), "cdo-speed-runs", time.strftime("%Y%m%d-%H%M%S") + f"-{os.getpid()}"))
    os.makedirs(outdir, exist_ok=True)
    log(f"outputs in {outdir}")

    t_start = time.monotonic()
    with open(os.path.join(outdir, "build.log"), "a") as logf:
        if args.base_exe:
            base_rev, base_exe, base_sha = args.base_exe, args.base_exe, "given-binary"
        else:
            base_rev = args.base or default_base(repo, args.head)
            base_exe, base_sha = cached_binary(repo, base_rev, logf)
        if args.head_exe:
            head_exe, head_label = args.head_exe, args.head_exe
        elif args.head:
            head_exe, head_sha = cached_binary(repo, args.head, logf)
            head_label = head_sha[:10]
        else:
            # TLS-34: the sources' key before the build, checked again after it.
            # One cdo-speed at a time builds into this folder, snapshots what it
            # built and copies its timed head (review r3-r4, sol: two concurrent
            # runs could swap the binary between one's build and its copies).
            os.makedirs(gate_release_dir(repo), exist_ok=True)
            with open(os.path.join(gate_release_dir(repo), ".cdo-speed-build.lock"),
                      "w") as build_lock:
                fcntl.flock(build_lock, fcntl.LOCK_EX)
                key_before = None if args.no_build else working_tree_key(repo)
                built = (os.path.join(gate_release_dir(repo), "release-fast", "alsem")
                         if args.no_build else build_working_tree(repo, logf))
                if not os.path.exists(built):
                    log(f"no binary at {built}")
                    return 2
                if not args.no_build:
                    record_tree_binary(repo, built, key_before)
                # A copy: a later build into the target folder cannot swap it mid-run.
                head_exe = os.path.join(outdir, "head-alsem")
                if not args.build_only:
                    shutil.copy2(built, head_exe)
            dirty = git(repo, "status", "--porcelain", "--untracked-files=no")
            head_label = git(repo, "rev-parse", "--short=10", "HEAD") + (" + edits" if dirty else "")
    t_built = time.monotonic()
    if args.build_only:
        log(f"built (no timing asked) in {t_built - t_start:.0f} s")
        return 0
    # ⟨TLS-104⟩ The baseline before the slot wait (review, Fable): a host that
    # sleeps while this waits for the slot or for builds is seen "before" the
    # first timed call, which then runs cold.
    global SUSPENDS
    SUSPENDS = Suspends()
    timed_hold(repo)
    global INSTR_EVENTS, INSTR_WHY
    INSTR_EVENTS, INSTR_WHY = instr_probe()
    if INSTR_EVENTS:
        log(f"instructions: counted per call ({', '.join(INSTR_EVENTS)}); advisory limit "
            f"x{INSTR_LIMIT}")
    else:
        log(f"instructions: NOT counted, CPU and wall only: {INSTR_WHY}")

    log(f"base {base_sha[:10]} ({base_rev}) against head {head_label}; workspace {ws}")
    hooks = [f"{k}={os.environ[k]}" for k in TEST_HOOKS if k in os.environ]
    if hooks:
        log(f"test hooks set (a real gate sets none): {' '.join(hooks)}")
    load0 = load_1min()
    pressure0, _ = cpu_pressure()
    records = []
    servers = {}
    # Per warm-up pass: (the host's pressure then, admitted only by the timeout).
    calm = []
    # Per timed block (each warm-up pass, the rounds): (name, its stall share).
    blocks = []
    cur = None
    # The peak RSS over every server pair (the warm-up passes start fresh ones).
    peaks = {"base": 0.0, "head": 0.0}
    # ⟨TLS-111⟩ The contradicted warm-up failures that added a flipped pair
    # (`contradicted`), and what still disagreed after it (HOST UNSTABLE).
    trigger, unstable, kept = [], [], set()
    passes = args.warm_samples
    rnd = 0
    try:
        p = 0
        while p < passes:
            if servers:
                # The previous pass's pair: a fresh one for this pass.
                for side, s in servers.items():
                    peaks[side] = max(peaks[side], s.peak_mib())
                    s.close()
                servers = {}
            calm.append(wait_calm(f"warm-up {p + 1} of {passes}"))
            # The pass's stall share also covers its untimed rebuild (TLS-79).
            cur = (f"warm-up {p + 1}", Stalls())
            exes = {"base": base_exe, "head": head_exe}
            first = "base" if p % 2 == 0 else "head"
            second = "head" if first == "base" else "base"
            log(f"round 0, warm-up {p + 1} of {passes} (fresh servers; "
                f"{first} builds first)")
            # ⟨TLS-79⟩ Each side's model build is timed with the other side's
            # server closed: with the first side's model resident (its server
            # idle, 0.00-0.01 s CPU), the second build cost x1.06-x1.29 CPU in
            # six pairs of one binary against itself, and x0.85-x0.99 with the
            # first server closed (2026-11-15). Then the first side is started
            # again and builds untimed, so the remaining warm-up calls and the
            # rounds have both servers.
            _, b_tool, b_args = WARM_UP[0]
            tag = f"r0.{p + 1}"
            servers[first] = Server(exes[first], ws, outdir, f"{first}{p + 1}")
            timed_call(servers[first], first, "build", b_tool, b_args, 0, p, tag, records)
            peaks[first] = max(peaks[first], servers[first].peak_mib())
            servers.pop(first).close()
            servers[second] = Server(exes[second], ws, outdir, f"{second}{p + 1}")
            timed_call(servers[second], second, "build", b_tool, b_args, 0, p, tag, records)
            servers[first] = Server(exes[first], ws, outdir, f"{first}{p + 1}-again")
            SUSPENDS.note(f"before {tag} build {first} again (untimed)")
            try:
                wall, cpu, _, code, err = servers[first].call(b_tool, b_args)
            finally:
                SUSPENDS.note(f"during {tag} build {first} again (untimed)")
            log(f"  {tag} {'build':<12} {first:<5} wall {wall:7.2f} s  cpu {cpu:7.2f} s"
                f"  (again, untimed: the server the remaining calls use)")
            if code != 0:
                raise CallFailed(f"{tag} build (again) on {first} exited {code}", err)
            run_round(0, servers, records, warm_pass=p, skip=1)
            blocks.append((cur[0], cur[1].share()))
            cur = None
            p += 1
            # ⟨TLS-111, note §5.2⟩ A judged warm-up row that fails while its two
            # passes disagree beyond CONTRADICTED_SPREAD: ONE more flipped pair
            # (passes 3 and 4, each side order once more, as passes 1 and 2),
            # never one pass (one order voting twice) and never a second pair.
            if p == passes == 2:
                after2 = judge_warm(records)
                fails2 = failing(after2)
                trigger = contradicted(fails2)
                if trigger:
                    passes = 4
                    kept = {r["call"] for r in after2 if r["judged"]}
                    log("a warm-up failure the passes contradict ("
                        + "; ".join(f"{c} {m} x{'/x'.join(f'{x:.3f}' for x in rs)}"
                                    for c, m, rs in trigger)
                        + f", beyond x{CONTRADICTED_SPREAD}): one more flipped pair")
        if trigger:
            # Every failure after pass 2 must agree within each side order, or
            # the four-pass mean could clear one the host decided.
            unstable = same_order_disagree(judge_warm(records, kept), fails2)
        # HOST UNSTABLE: nothing the rounds measure changes that verdict.
        if not unstable:
            cur = ("rounds", Stalls())
            while True:
                rnd += 1
                run_round(rnd, servers, records)
                if rnd < args.rounds:
                    continue
                _, noisy = judge([dict(r) for r in records if r["round"] > 0])
                if noisy and rnd < args.max_rounds:
                    log(f"the rounds disagree about {', '.join(noisy)}: one more round")
                    continue
                break
            blocks.append((cur[0], cur[1].share()))
            cur = None
        for side in servers:
            peaks[side] = max(peaks[side], servers[side].peak_mib())
    except CallFailed as e:
        what, err = e.args
        if cur is not None:
            blocks.append((cur[0], cur[1].share()))
        log(f"FAIL: {what}; nothing is judged on a failed call")
        for line in err or ["(no stderr)"]:
            log(f"  | {line}")
        label = under_load_label(calm, blocks) + SUSPENDS.label()
        if label:
            log(f"FAIL{label}")
        return 1
    finally:
        for s in servers.values():
            s.close()

    # ⟨ENG-110⟩ The IR cache rows: untimed fills, reported builds; a label, never a failure.
    try:
        ir_label = ir_build_rows({"base": base_exe, "head": head_exe}, ws, outdir,
                                 args.ir_cold)
    except (Exception, SystemExit) as e:
        # Anything here (a server that cannot start or answer, a dead pipe) is a label,
        # never the verdict: the judged timing is done (Fable r1).
        ir_label = f" IR ROWS NOT RUN ({type(e).__name__}: {e.args[0] if e.args else e})"

    round_records = [r for r in records if r["round"] > 0]
    rows, noisy = judge(round_records) if round_records else ([], [])
    warm_rows = judge_warm(records, kept)
    # `cpu_advisory`: CPU failures made advisory by the owner's decision (TLS-111).
    failed, advisories, cpu_advisories = [], [], []

    def cpu_fails(row, text):
        advisory = cpu_advisory(row)
        if (not advisory and row.get("partial")
                and cpu_advisory(dict(row, partial=None, partial_rounds=None))):
            # ⟨TLS-130 reviews r2 and r3⟩ Said only when the partial count is
            # what withheld an advisory the row would otherwise get.
            text += f" (no CPU advisory: a count may be partial: {row['partial']})"
        (cpu_advisories if advisory else failed).append(advisory or text)
    log(f"verdict, head/base (limits: rounds cpu {CPU_LIMIT} and wall {WALL_LIMIT} on the "
        f"median ratio, warm-up cpu {WARM_CPU_LIMIT} and wall {WARM_WALL_LIMIT}, memory "
        f"{MEM_LIMIT}; a call is judged "
        f"alone when its base CPU is at least {MIN_SECONDS} s):")
    for row in warm_rows + rows:
        c, w = row["cpu"], row["wall"]
        warm = row.get("warm", False)
        note = "" if row["judged"] else (
            "  (reported only)" if warm else "  (in the round total only)")
        if row["call"] in noisy:
            note += "  (rounds disagree: the median decides)"
        if not row["same_output"] and row["call"] != "round-total":
            note += "  [outputs or exit codes differ]"
        if warm:
            note += (f"  (x{'/x'.join(f'{x:.3f}' for x in c['ratios'])} by pass; load "
                     f"{row['load']['min']:.1f}-{row['load']['max']:.1f})")
        i = row.get("instr")
        instr_col = f"   instr x{i['ratio']:.4f}" if i else ""
        # ⟨TLS-111⟩ The sides' clocks (GHz) and their cycles-per-instruction ratio.
        if "clock" in row:
            instr_col += (f"   clock {row['clock']['base']:.2f}/{row['clock']['head']:.2f} GHz"
                          f" x{row['clock']['ratio']:.3f}")
        if "cpi" in row:
            instr_col += f"   cpi x{row['cpi']['ratio']:.3f}"
        log(f"  {row['call']:<14} cpu {c['base']:7.2f} -> {c['head']:7.2f} s x{c['ratio']:.3f}"
            f"   wall {w['base']:7.2f} -> {w['head']:7.2f} s x{w['ratio']:.3f}"
            f"{instr_col}   spread {c['spread']:.2f}/{w['spread']:.2f}{note}")
        if not row["judged"]:
            continue
        # ⟨TLS-83⟩ Advisory: reported, never failing (see INSTR_LIMIT), on the
        # judged rows only: below the CPU-time cutoff a call is a few thousand
        # instructions whose ratio swings (8 false advisories in a run of one
        # binary against itself when tried on every row; review r1, sol).
        if i and i["ratio"] > INSTR_LIMIT:
            advisories.append(f"{row['call']}: instructions x{i['ratio']:.4f} "
                              f"(advisory limit {INSTR_LIMIT}{', warm-up' if warm else ''})")
        if warm:
            if c["ratio"] > WARM_CPU_LIMIT:
                cpu_fails(row, f"{row['call']}: CPU time x{c['ratio']:.2f} "
                               f"(limit {WARM_CPU_LIMIT}, warm-up, {len(c['ratios'])} pass(es))")
            if w["ratio"] > WARM_WALL_LIMIT:
                failed.append(f"{row['call']}: wall time x{w['ratio']:.2f} "
                              f"(limit {WARM_WALL_LIMIT}, warm-up, {len(c['ratios'])} pass(es); "
                              f"lost parallelism?)")
            continue
        if c["ratio"] > CPU_LIMIT:
            cpu_fails(row, f"{row['call']}: CPU time x{c['ratio']:.2f} (limit {CPU_LIMIT})")
        if w["ratio"] > WALL_LIMIT:
            failed.append(f"{row['call']}: wall time x{w['ratio']:.2f} (limit {WALL_LIMIT})")
    mem_ratio = peaks["head"] / peaks["base"] if peaks["base"] else 9.9
    log(f"  {'peak memory':<14} {peaks['base']:.0f} -> {peaks['head']:.0f} MiB x{mem_ratio:.3f}"
        " (every server process's VmHWM, summed)")
    if mem_ratio > MEM_LIMIT:
        failed.append(f"peak memory x{mem_ratio:.2f} (limit {MEM_LIMIT})")
    # ⟨TLS-83⟩ The instruction ratios on the verdict line (cdo-gate's summary
    # quotes it): the model build (warm-up) and the round total.
    if INSTR_EVENTS:
        shown = [f"{r['call']} x{r['instr']['ratio']:.4f}"
                 for r in warm_rows + rows if r["call"] in ("build", "round-total") and "instr" in r]
        # ⟨TLS-111⟩ A row has a ratio only when every call of it was counted on
        # both sides: say how many calls were, so one uncounted call does not
        # read as none (an A/A run counted 55 of 64 and said "no call fully counted").
        n_counted = sum(1 for r in records if r.get("instr"))
        none_shown = ("no call fully counted" if not n_counted
                      else "build and round-total not fully counted")
        instr_text = (f"instructions: {', '.join(shown) or none_shown}; "
                      f"advisory limit x{INSTR_LIMIT}"
                      f"{', ADVISORY: ' + str(len(advisories)) + ' over' if advisories else ''}"
                      + (f"; {n_counted} of {len(records)} calls counted"
                         if 0 < n_counted < len(records) else ""))
    else:
        instr_text = "instructions: not counted, CPU and wall only"
    for line in advisories + cpu_advisories:
        log(f"ADVISORY (not failing): {line}")
    elapsed = time.monotonic() - t_start
    load_label = under_load_label(calm, blocks)
    flags = clock_cpi_flags(warm_rows + rows)
    label = load_label + SUSPENDS.label() + clock_cpi_label(flags) + ir_label
    if cpu_advisories:
        label += (f" CPU ADVISORY ({len(cpu_advisories)}: equal instructions, a CPU-only "
                  f"failure that head's lower clock explains, owner decision "
                  f"{CPU_ADVISORY_QUESTION}): "
                  + "; ".join(cpu_advisories))
    # ⟨TLS-111⟩ The extra pair, said in the verdict (note §5.2).
    if trigger:
        why = "; ".join(f"{c} {m} x{'/x'.join(f'{x:.3f}' for x in rs[:2])}"
                        for c, m, rs in trigger)
        label += (f" (one more flipped warm-up pair: a failure its passes contradicted "
                  f"beyond x{CONTRADICTED_SPREAD}: {why}; "
                  + (f"the same-order passes still disagree: {'; '.join(unstable)})" if unstable
                     else "the same-order passes agree, judged on 4 passes)"))
    under = [i + 1 for i, (_, loaded) in enumerate(calm) if loaded]
    unknown = any(loaded is None for _, loaded in calm) or any(s is None for _, s in blocks)
    # ⟨TLS-111 review r1⟩ HOST UNSTABLE is in `failed` too, so a reader that
    # checks `not failed` never takes it for a pass; `verdict` says which.
    if unstable:
        failed.append(f"HOST UNSTABLE: the same-order warm-up passes still disagree beyond "
                      f"x{CONTRADICTED_SPREAD}: {'; '.join(unstable)} (cannot run, exit 2)")
    verdict = "HOST UNSTABLE" if unstable else "FAIL" if failed else "PASS"
    with open(os.path.join(outdir, "speed.json"), "w") as f:
        json.dump(dict(verdict=verdict, base=base_sha, head=head_label, workspace=ws, rounds=rnd,
                       limits=dict(cpu=CPU_LIMIT, wall=WALL_LIMIT, mem=MEM_LIMIT,
                                   warm_cpu=WARM_CPU_LIMIT, warm_wall=WARM_WALL_LIMIT,
                                   min_seconds=MIN_SECONDS, warm_min_seconds=WARM_MIN_SECONDS),
                       build_s=t_built - t_start, timing_s=elapsed - (t_built - t_start),
                       peaks_mib=peaks, warm_rows=warm_rows, rows=rows, records=records,
                       failed=failed, loadavg_start=load0, loadavg_end=load_1min(),
                       pressure_start=pressure0, pressure_end=cpu_pressure()[0],
                       calm_pressure=CALM_PRESSURE, admitted_by_timeout=under,
                       stall_shares=dict(blocks),
                       under_load=bool(load_label.startswith(" UNDER")),
                       suspended=[dict(where=w, seconds=s) for w, s in SUSPENDS.seen],
                       # The warm-up passes run (4 after an extra pair), and
                       # the passes asked (--warm-samples).
                       pressure_unknown=unknown, warm_samples=passes,
                       warm_samples_asked=args.warm_samples,
                       advisory=cpu_advisories, clock_band=CLOCK_BAND, cpi_band=CPI_BAND,
                       clock_labels=flags["clock"], cpi_labels=flags["cpi"],
                       contradicted_spread=CONTRADICTED_SPREAD,
                       extra_pair=[dict(call=c, metric=m, ratios=rs) for c, m, rs in trigger],
                       host_unstable=unstable,
                       instructions=dict(events=list(INSTR_EVENTS), why_not=INSTR_WHY,
                                         advisory_limit=INSTR_LIMIT, advisories=advisories)),
                  f, indent=1)
    log(f"build {t_built - t_start:.0f} s, timing {elapsed - (t_built - t_start):.0f} s, "
        f"load average {load0:.1f} at the start, {load_1min():.1f} at the end; "
        f"CPU stall share of this container: {shares_text(blocks)}")
    # ⟨TLS-111⟩ Still contradicted after the extra pair: the host, not the
    # change, decided the timings. Cannot run (exit 2), never a PASS; no rounds ran.
    # Every failure is printed, a HOST UNSTABLE run's too (a memory failure stays one).
    for line in failed:
        log(f"FAIL: {line}")
    if unstable:
        log(f"HOST UNSTABLE{label}; {instr_text}")
        return 2
    if failed:
        # After the failures, not in parentheses: it must not read as the reason.
        log(f"FAIL{label}; {instr_text}")
        return 1
    log(f"PASS{label} ({instr_text})")
    return 0


if __name__ == "__main__":
    try:
        sys.exit(main())
    except SystemExit as e:
        if isinstance(e.code, str):  # a "cannot run" message
            print(e.code, file=sys.stderr, flush=True)
            # ⟨TLS-104⟩ A run stopped after the timing began still says the
            # host slept (review, sol): a call that timed out across a suspend.
            if SUSPENDS is not None and SUSPENDS.seen:
                print(f"cdo-speed:{SUSPENDS.label()}", file=sys.stderr, flush=True)
            sys.exit(2)
        raise
